import Foundation

// The five analysis tools, ported from the Android client's `core/.../tools/` package, which in
// turn ports the platform's own `src/tools/definitions/`. Every prompt body below was lifted
// mechanically rather than retyped and is pinned by the golden fixtures in
// `Tests/EmperorCoreTests/Resources/tools/`, generated by running the platform's JavaScript.

// MARK: - Devil's Advocate

/// Devil's Advocate — attack your own draft before the other side does.
///
/// Ported from `emperor-ai/src/tools/definitions/blindSpots.js`. The two sweeps below are the
/// subject-matter value of the tool and were extracted mechanically rather than retyped; the
/// platform's own note calls them "a floor the model must clear, not a menu — an advocate stops
/// trusting an adversarial review the moment it misses the objection that a junior on the other
/// side would have found."
///
/// ## Why the whole prompt branches
///
/// A contract under negotiation has no Court, no relief and no hearing, so a severity scale built
/// on "dismissed at the threshold" is noise on a draft NDA. Every clause that names the adversary
/// therefore has two forms, chosen by ``isContractKind(_:)``.
private let CONTRACT_KIND =
    "contract|agreement|deed|mou|memorandum of understanding|lease|licen[cs]e|nda|" +
    "term sheet|purchase order|policy|tender"

private let LITIGATION_KIND =
    "plaint|petition|writ|suit|appeal|revision|complaint|application|bail|quash|counter|" +
    "statement of defence|notice"

/// Kotlin's `Regex.containsMatchIn`, case-insensitively.
private func containsMatchIn(_ pattern: String, _ text: String) -> Bool {
    text.range(of: pattern, options: [.regularExpression, .caseInsensitive]) != nil
}

/// Which sweep a document type attracts.
///
/// The dropdown is free-text at the edges, so this is decided by what the value looks like. In
/// Indian practice "agreement", "tender", "policy" and "licence" are litigation words at least as
/// often as transactional ones — "Writ Petition challenging cancellation of tender", "Suit for
/// specific performance of an agreement to sell" — and routing those into the clause sweep costs
/// the advocate limitation, Order VII Rule 11 and verification, which is the whole reason they
/// opened this tool. So **a litigation marker anywhere wins outright**, and a tie or an
/// unrecognised value falls to the litigation sweep.
func isContractKind(_ kind: String) -> Bool {
    containsMatchIn(CONTRACT_KIND, kind) && !containsMatchIn(LITIGATION_KIND, kind)
}

private let LITIGATION_SWEEP = """
- LIMITATION — IDENTIFY THE REGIME BEFORE NAMING A PERIOD: the Limitation Act, 1963 does not govern every forum. A consumer complaint runs on Section 69 of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (two years from the cause of action); a challenge to an award runs on Section 34(3) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 (three months, extendable by thirty days on sufficient cause and not thereafter, Section 5 of the Limitation Act being expressly excluded — Union of India v. Popular Construction Co.); an appeal to the NCLAT on Section 61 IBC (thirty days plus fifteen); a writ attracts no Article at all, only delay and laches. Name the governing regime first, then run it: date of accrual against date of filing, the Article or provision that actually applies, exclusions (Section 12, and the statutory notice period under Section 15(2)), acknowledgement in writing under Section 18 and part-payment under Section 19. On an appeal or an application, ask whether condonation under Section 5 is pleaded and supported by an application. On a SUIT there is no Section 5 route at all — never recommend one; the only answers are the Sections 12 to 15 exclusions, Section 14, an acknowledgement under Section 18 or a part-payment under Section 19, and if the plaint is out of time on its own averments the exposure is rejection under Order VII Rule 11(d) CPC.
- CAUSE OF ACTION: are the constituent facts pleaded, does the pleading disclose a cause of action at all, when and where did it arise, and is a bare assertion doing the work that a pleaded fact should be doing.
- JURISDICTION: territorial (Sections 16 to 20 CPC), pecuniary and subject-matter; an exclusive-jurisdiction or arbitration clause in the instrument sued upon (a Section 8 reference under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996); statutory ouster (Section 430 of the Companies Act, 2013, Section 34 SARFAESI, RERA, the NCLT / DRT / consumer overlap); and whether the jurisdiction paragraph pleads the facts that found jurisdiction or merely asserts it.
- MAINTAINABILITY BEFORE THIS FORUM: for a writ — alternative efficacious remedy, disputed questions of fact, laches, suppression in the affidavit, a contractual claim dressed up as a writ, and whether the respondent is amenable to Article 226 at all. That is NOT the Article 12 test: under Article 226 a writ lies against any person or authority discharging a public function or a public duty (Andi Mukta Sadguru Trust v. V.R. Rudani; Binny Ltd. v. V. Sadasivan), which is why writs run against private aided institutions and bodies performing statutory functions that are not "State" — keep Article 12 for Article 32. For an appeal or revision — appealability of the order, Order XLIII Rule 1 or a substantial question of law under Section 100 CPC, limitation and leave; before the NCLT or NCLAT — the threshold amount, a pre-existing dispute under Section 9 IBC, locus and the correct Bench; for a consumer complaint — the definition of "consumer" and the commercial-purpose exclusion, and the pecuniary slab; on the criminal side — the Bhajan Lal parameters for quashing, sanction to prosecute, cognizance and limitation.
- WHICH CRIMINAL CODE GOVERNS: the BNSS, BNS and BSA, 2023 apply to proceedings instituted on or after 01.07.2024, but by Section 531 BNSS any appeal, application, trial, inquiry or investigation PENDING on that date continues under the CrPC, 1973 as if the BNSS had not come into force. Fix the applicable code from the dates on the record before citing any number, state which you have applied, and give the provision in both codes — quashing under Section 482 CrPC / Section 528 BNSS, sanction under Section 197 CrPC / Section 218 BNSS, a perjury reference under Section 340 CrPC / Section 379 BNSS. Where you cannot fix the corresponding number with confidence, name the provision by its subject and the code you are in rather than guessing a number.
- PARTIES: non-joinder of a necessary party (Order I Rule 9 CPC), misjoinder, suing a division or a brand name instead of the juristic entity, legal representatives of a deceased party, minor and next friend, notice under Section 80 CPC where the Government or a public officer is arrayed, and the authority behind the signature — board resolution, power of attorney, vakalatnama, and the competence of the deponent to swear to these facts.
- WANT OF PLEADING: trace every relief in the prayer back to a foundational averment in the body. A relief prayed but never pleaded is not granted and a case not pleaded is not heard. Fraud, misrepresentation, coercion and undue influence need particulars under Order VI Rule 4 CPC; readiness and willingness must be pleaded in terms under Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act, 1963.
- UNSUPPORTED AVERMENTS: which material assertions have no document, affidavit or witness behind them on this record. For each, name the document that must be obtained and from whom. Identify the averments that collapse the moment the other side simply puts them to strict proof.
- ADMISSIONS AND CARELESS CONCESSIONS: what this pleading gives away — an acknowledgement of liability, an admission of receipt or of notice, adoption of the other side's version of a term, a concession on the date of knowledge or on jurisdiction, an over-claim that invites costs or a perjury reference under Section 340 CrPC / Section 379 BNSS. Quote each one in the words used.
- CONTRADICTIONS WITHIN THE RECORD: pleading against annexure, pleading against the reply to the legal notice or against an earlier affidavit, prayer against valuation, figures that do not add up, dates that do not reconcile. This is where the party's own documents defeat the party, and it is the first thing competent opposing counsel looks for.
- ORDER VII RULE 11 CPC: run clauses (a) to (f) one by one and give the status of each.
- VERIFICATION AND AFFIDAVIT: the verification under Order VI Rule 15 CPC, the supporting affidavit, the Statement of Truth under Order VI Rule 15A in a commercial suit, which paragraphs are sworn to knowledge and which to information and belief, attestation, and a separate affidavit for each interim application.
- VALUATION AND COURT FEE: valuation for jurisdiction against valuation for court fee, ad valorem fee where a declaration carries a consequential relief, deficit fee, and any relief under-valued to fit the forum.
- DOCUMENTS AND EVIDENCE: filing with the plaint under Order VII Rule 14 or losing the right to produce, the list of reliance, originals and certified copies, the certificate under Section 65B of the Evidence Act / Section 63 of the BSA for every electronic document, and the stamping and registration of the instrument sued upon — an insufficiently stamped instrument is inadmissible under Section 35 of the Stamp Act.
- INTERIM RELIEF: prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable injury pleaded as three distinct things rather than asserted in a single sentence; suppression of a material fact, which is fatal on the equity side; delay in approaching the Court; and the undertaking as to damages.
- BARS AND PARALLEL PROCEEDINGS: res judicata, Order II Rule 2 CPC where a relief was omitted in an earlier suit, a Section 10 CPC stay, splitting of claims, undisclosed parallel or previous proceedings, and forum shopping.
- THE RELIEF ITSELF: is the prayer executable as worded, is it vague, is it wider than the pleaded case, does it ask for a mandatory injunction the Court will not grant at this stage, and what will the Court actually be in a position to give.
- ADVERSE AUTHORITY: name the binding judgment that cuts against this case and state what it holds — the case the other side opens with. If a proposition relied on has been overruled or doubted, say so. Cite only real, verifiable authorities; where you are not certain a citation is accurate, state the proposition and mark the citation as requiring verification rather than producing a plausible-looking one.
"""

private let CONTRACT_SWEEP = """
- RECIPROCITY: every obligation that runs one way only — which promises, service standards, notice periods, audit rights, termination triggers and remedies bind our client alone, and what the mirror clause should say.
- LIABILITY: is liability capped at all, is the cap symmetric, do the carve-outs swallow the cap, is indirect and consequential loss excluded on both sides, and is an uncapped indemnity sitting alongside a capped liability clause and quietly defeating it.
- INDEMNITY: who indemnifies whom and for what, conduct and control of a claim, notification and cooperation conditions, interaction with insurance, and survival after termination.
- TERMINATION AND CURE: termination for convenience and on whose side, cure periods, whether a single breach triggers immediate termination, and the consequences on termination — transition assistance, refund of amounts paid in advance, licence and data reversion, and which clauses survive.
- DISPUTE RESOLUTION, TESTED FOR WORKABILITY: seat as distinct from venue; unilateral appointment by one party (the Perkins Eastman and TRF line); an even number of arbitrators — Section 10 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 provides for an odd number, but it is a derogable provision and a two-member tribunal is not void: Narayan Prasad Lohia v. Nikunj Kumar Lohia, (2002) 3 SCC 572, holds the objection waived unless taken under Section 16, so flag it as a live risk of deadlock and an avoidable preliminary fight, not as invalidity; no institution or rules named; a pre-arbitral step drafted as a condition precedent; an exclusive-jurisdiction clause sitting inconsistently with the arbitration clause; and governing law, seat and jurisdiction pointing in three different directions.
- STAMPING AND REGISTRATION: adequacy of stamp duty under the law of the State of execution, and the impounding and inadmissibility of an under-stamped instrument until duty and penalty are paid (Section 35, Indian Stamp Act, 1899, and the corresponding State enactment). Registration where the instrument is compulsorily registrable — a lease from year to year, for a term exceeding one year, or reserving a yearly rent: Section 17(1)(d) of the Registration Act, 1908 r/w Section 107 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, with the consequence of non-registration under Section 49 (it does not affect the immovable property and is not receivable as evidence of the transaction, subject to the collateral-purpose proviso). The threshold is ONE YEAR: an eleven-month leave and licence is the drafting convention that keeps an instrument below it, not a defect — do not flag one as unregistered on that ground; look instead at the actual term, at renewal or a lock-in that carries it past a year, and at any reservation of yearly rent. Also execution formalities, e-signature and the authority of the signatory.
- MONEY: interest on delayed payment, the consequences under the MSMED Act, 2006 if either party is a registered micro or small enterprise, one-way set-off, taxes and gross-up, price escalation, and security by way of bank guarantee, deposit or retention.
- RESTRICTIVE COVENANTS AND IP: a post-termination non-compete, void under Section 27 of the Contract Act, 1872; the drafting of non-solicit; the term and survival of confidentiality; ownership of IP, licence-back, rights in pre-existing material and in deliverables.
- CONTROL EVENTS: assignment and change of control, novation, subcontracting, exclusivity, minimum commitments, and auto-renewal or deemed-acceptance clauses that turn silence into agreement.
- FORCE MAJEURE AND CHANGE IN LAW: what qualifies, whose obligations are suspended, the long-stop and the right to walk away, and whether payment obligations are excused.
- BOILERPLATE THAT BITES: an entire-agreement clause wiping out the pre-contract assurances the deal was actually done on, a notices clause with a wrong address or excluding email, a clause shortening the limitation period, unilateral amendment rights, and waiver-by-conduct language.
- DRAFTING DEFECTS: defined terms used but never defined, broken cross-references, schedules that contradict the operative clauses, "reasonable efforts" doing the work that "shall" should do, and any ambiguity that will be read contra proferentem against whoever drafted it.
"""

/// Devil's Advocate.
let BlindSpotsTool = ToolSpec(
    id: "blind-spots",
    title: "Devil's Advocate",
    short: "Adversarial self-review",
    blurb: "Attack your own draft before the other side does — ranked vulnerabilities, "
        + "pin-cited, each with a fix.",
    // `riskReport`, not `document`. This is a diagnostic OF a deliverable, never the deliverable
    // itself — rendering it as a document would put Ready-to-File buttons on a critique of the
    // user's own draft, which is actively misleading.
    output: .riskReport,
    inputs: [
        ToolField(
            key: "docKind",
            label: "What am I attacking",
            type: .select,
            options: [
                "Plaint / Civil Suit",
                "Writ Petition",
                "Written Statement / Counter-affidavit",
                "Appeal / Revision",
                "Interim Application (injunction, stay, attachment)",
                "Petition before NCLT / NCLAT",
                "Arbitration Claim / Statement of Defence",
                "Bail / Quashing / Criminal Petition",
                "Consumer Complaint",
                "Legal Notice / Reply to Notice",
                "Contract / Agreement",
                "The whole case, not one document",
            ]
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "side",
            label: "You act for",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "e.g. the Petitioner / the Buyer / Accused No. 2"
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "material",
            label: "Paste the draft to attack (or attach it above)",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "Paste your pleading, application or contract… "
                + "(leave blank if you have attached it)",
            big: true
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "worry",
            label: "Where you already feel exposed",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "e.g. Limitation — the notice went out three years after the last "
                + "invoice. (optional)"
        ),
    ],
    build: buildBlindSpots
)

func buildBlindSpots(_ v: ToolValues, _ today: Date) -> String {
    let kind = v.text("docKind", "")
    let isContract = isContractKind(kind)
    let subject = kind.isEmpty ? "the material supplied" : "the \(kind)"
    let acting = v.text("side", "")

    // Single-quoted in the source, not templates — and that difference matters: an earlier
    // extraction that assumed every branch was a template silently gave these two the NEXT
    // declaration's text and shifted every variable below them. The golden fixtures caught it.
    let sweepHeading = isContract ? "CLAUSE SWEEP" : "THRESHOLD SWEEP"
    let actionHeading = isContract ? "WHAT TO RENEGOTIATE, IN ORDER" : "WHAT TO FIX BEFORE FILING"

    let actingNote = acting.isEmpty ? "" : """
The advocate acts for \(acting). "The other side" therefore means every party opposed to \(acting), and their counsel is who you are for the next few minutes.

"""

    let trackScopeNote = isContract ? "" : """
SCOPE THE SWEEP TO THE TRACK FIRST. The list below is written around a civil suit. Decide which checks this forum and this document actually attract and DROP the inapplicable blocks out of the sweep table altogether rather than listing them as Clear — on a bail or quashing petition, a consumer complaint, an NCLT petition, an arbitration pleading or a legal notice, the civil-suit rows (valuation and court fee, Order VII Rule 11 clauses (a) to (f), the Order VI Rule 15A Statement of Truth, Order II Rule 2) have no application, and "Valuation and court fee — Clear" on a bail petition is both false and noise. Keep the row only where applicability is genuinely borderline, and mark it Not applicable with the reason. Then ADD the checks peculiar to this track that the list does not carry — the charge-sheet clock and default bail on the criminal side, the Section 8 demand notice and a pre-existing dispute before the NCLT, Section 69 of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 and the commercial-purpose exclusion before a consumer forum, Section 21 invocation and the arbitrability of the claim in an arbitration.

"""

    let worryNote = v.has("worry") ? """

WHERE THE ADVOCATE ALREADY FEELS EXPOSED:
\(v.text("worry", ""))
Deal with each of these head-on and early — confirm it, or explain precisely why it is not the problem they think it is. Then keep going: the findings that matter most are the ones they have not thought of.
""" : ""

    let material = v.text(
        "material",
        "(nothing pasted — use the attached record if one is present; if none is present, follow the NO RECORD instruction above)"
    )

    let twoReadings = isContract
        ? """
first as the counterparty's counsel marking this instrument up for their own client — hunting for every right this drafting hands them and every obligation it fails to impose on them; and second as that same counsel two years later, drafting their notice of claim on this instrument and working out which clause they will invoke and which of our client's positions the drafting has already conceded
"""
        : """
first as opposing counsel briefed to defeat it, preferably at the threshold and without ever reaching the merits; and second as the Hon'ble Court looking for the shortest defensible reason to reject it, dismiss it or refuse the relief sought
"""

    let consequenceLine = isContract
        ? """
what actually happens — the clause held void or unenforceable, the instrument impounded and inadmissible until duty and penalty are paid, an uncapped indemnity called for a sum larger than the contract value, termination exercised against our client on thirty days' notice, a payment obligation enforceable with no defence available, or a dispute clause that puts our client before a forum it cannot fight in.
"""
        : """
what actually happens — rejection under Order VII Rule 11 CPC, dismissal at the admission stage, refusal of interim relief, the claim cut down by a specific amount, costs, or a finding the client will live with for years.
"""

    let readingOrder = isContract
        ? """
the definitions clause; then the operative obligations and the service or delivery standards; then payment, liability, indemnity and termination; then the dispute-resolution clause; then the schedules, annexures and side letters read AGAINST the operative clauses; then the stamp endorsement and the execution page.
"""
        : """
(a) the prayer clause, because every finding below is measured against what is actually asked for; (b) the verification, the affidavit and the Statement of Truth; (c) the paragraphs pleading the cause of action, the date of accrual and limitation; (d) the cause title, the memo of parties and the jurisdiction paragraph; (e) the list of annexures; (f) then the annexures themselves, read AGAINST the pleading rather than on their own — that is where the contradictions are.
"""

    let leadSection = isContract
        ? """
1. <h2>THE STRONGEST POSITION YOU HAVE HANDED THEM</h2> — the single clause, omission or asymmetry the counterparty will rely on when this instrument is in dispute, written in their voice, as their counsel would put it in a notice of claim, in four to six sentences. Then, under a bold "Can it be resisted as drafted?", the best answer available on the face of this instrument as it stands — or a plain statement that as drafted it cannot be resisted, and the specific amendment that would answer it. This goes first because it is the one thing the advocate least wants to hear and most needs to.
"""
        : """
1. <h2>THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT AGAINST YOU</h2> — the single submission the other side will actually lead with, written in their voice, as they would put it to the Hon'ble Court, in four to six sentences. Then, under a bold "Can it be answered?", the best answer available on THIS record — or a plain statement that on this record it cannot be answered, and what would be needed to answer it. This goes first because it is the one thing the advocate least wants to hear and most needs to.
"""

    let verdictSection = isContract
        ? """
2. <h2>VERDICT</h2> — a short, honest paragraph answering one question: would you sign this as drafted and unamended — Yes / Only with changes / No. Then the worst clause in it, and what this instrument really turns on for our client. Close with a one-line count of how many Fatal, Serious, Material and Minor findings follow.
"""
        : """
2. <h2>VERDICT</h2> — a short, honest paragraph: does this survive a threshold challenge (Yes / Doubtful / No), what is the weakest link, and what the matter really turns on. Close with a one-line count of how many Fatal, Serious, Material and Minor findings follow.
"""

    let useLine = isContract
        ? """
<strong>How they will use it</strong> — the notice, the claim or the negotiating position it becomes in the counterparty's hands
"""
        : """
<strong>How they will use it</strong> — the application, preliminary objection or line of cross-examination it becomes in the other side's hands
"""

    let severityLadder = isContract
        ? """
FATAL: the clause or the instrument is void or unenforceable, or inadmissible for want of stamp, or it leaves exposure uncapped and capable of exceeding the whole contract value — do not sign it in this form. SERIOUS: it costs real money or surrenders a right that is not recovered anywhere else in the instrument — a termination right running one way, an indemnity outside the cap, a payment obligation with no corresponding remedy — and it must be renegotiated. MATERIAL: the counterparty will use it in a dispute or at the next renewal and it will cost ground; press for it. MINOR: drafting hygiene — correct it in the mark-up, but do not spend leverage on it.
"""
        : """
FATAL: the matter can be ended on this ground without a hearing on the merits. SERIOUS: the relief is likely to be refused or materially cut down. MATERIAL: it will be exploited in argument or in cross-examination and will cost ground. MINOR: correct it, but nothing turns on it.
"""

    let noRecordFallback = isContract
        ? """
the ranked ways an instrument of this kind is most often turned against the party who signed it, each with the check the advocate should run against their own draft
"""
        : """
the ranked grounds on which such a filing is most often defeated in Indian practice, each with the check the advocate should run against their own draft
"""

    return """
Adversarial self-review of the advocate's OWN work. You are not their ally on this task.

For this task you have been retained by the OTHER SIDE. Read \(subject) twice: \(twoReadings). Report what those two readings find.
\(actingNote)
RULES OF ENGAGEMENT, and they are not optional:
- Do not validate, reassure or compliment. "This is well drafted" is not a finding, and no part of this output is an appreciation of the draft. VERDICT is the only place where you state an overall position.
- Do not hedge a real defect into nothing. Write "this is barred" and not "this may arguably be perceived as potentially problematic".
- Never manufacture a weakness to fill space. A defect you invented wastes the advocate's time exactly as a defect you missed does. If a check comes back genuinely sound, record it as Clear in the sweep and move on.
- State every exposure as a consequence and not as a category: \(consequenceLine)

READ THE RECORD IN THIS ORDER, before writing anything: \(readingOrder)
On a long paperbook you will not read every page. Read the parts named above in full, then read the pages you intend to cite, and disclose the extent of the sweep in the COVERAGE line. Never present a partial read as a complete one — a clean report is taken by the advocate to mean nothing was found, so silence about what you did not read is the worst failure this tool can commit.

ANCHOR EVERY FINDING TO THE RECORD:
- For a defect in what IS written: quote the offending words verbatim (up to about 25 words) and pin-cite them — document name and page, and the paragraph, clause or exhibit number. A criticism you cannot anchor to specific words is not a finding; locate it or drop it.
- WHEN THERE IS NO PAGE TO CITE, SAY SO INSTEAD OF SUPPLYING ONE. If you cannot establish the page, cite the paragraph or clause and write "page not determinable" — do NOT invent a page number, a clause number or a document name. Where the material was PASTED into the form rather than attached there is no pagination at all: cite by paragraph or clause number and by the quoted words alone, attribute it to "(pasted material)", and record that once in the COVERAGE line. A pin-cite to a page you have not read is worse than no pin-cite at all, because it is the one error that gets discovered in open Court.
- Indian records arrive as phone photographs and poor scans. Where the source text is garbled, quote what is actually on the page and mark it [text unclear in source] — never tidy the wording silently, because a finding built on words the document does not contain collapses the moment the page is produced. For an illegible figure or date, give your best reading followed by [figure unclear in source], do not rest a Fatal or Serious finding on it alone, and treat an unreadable page as a gap for WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW.
- For a defect in what is NOT written: label it [OMISSION] and state exactly where it should have appeared — for example, "no averment of readiness and willingness anywhere in paragraphs 1 to 24 of the plaint, required by Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act, 1963".
- Where a finding turns on a fact you cannot verify from the record, neither guess it nor suppress the finding. State it conditionally with the test the advocate must run: "if the notice was served after 14.03.2023 the suit is time-barred — verify against the acknowledgement due card".
- Never import a date, figure, party name, case number or citation that is not in the record or supplied below.

\(trackScopeNote)RUN EVERY CHECK BELOW THAT APPLIES, and report each one in the sweep table even when it comes back clear. Treat the list as a floor, not a ceiling — if the record throws up a vulnerability that is not on it, that is the one worth having.
\(isContract ? CONTRACT_SWEEP : LITIGATION_SWEEP)

STRUCTURE THE CANVAS EXACTLY AS FOLLOWS, in this order, with an <h2> for each section heading and a <table> wherever one is specified.

Open with a single COVERAGE line before Section 1, not as a section: name each document actually read and the page range covered in it, and say plainly where the material was pasted rather than attached, or where no record was supplied at all. The advocate must be able to see the extent of the sweep before reading a word of the findings.

\(leadSection)

\(verdictSection)

3. <h2>VULNERABILITIES, RANKED</h2> — first a <table> with columns: # | Severity | Vulnerability | Where in the record | Fix (one line), ordered strictly worst first. Then one <h3> per finding, numbered to match the table, each containing in bold-led lines: <strong>Where</strong> — the quoted words with the pin-cite, or [OMISSION] and where it should have been; <strong>Why it hurts</strong> — the consequence, with the provision or authority that produces it; \(useLine); <strong>FIX</strong>.
   Severity has four values and each means something specific. \(severityLadder) If everything is marked FATAL the ranking is worthless and the advocate will ignore the whole report, so reserve it for what earns it.
   FIX must be operative and not advisory. Give the actual replacement or additional wording, drafted to \(isContract ? "execution" : "filing") standard and ready to paste in. Where the fix is evidentiary rather than a matter of drafting, name the exact document or affidavit to be obtained, from whom, and by when. A finding without a fix is just anxiety.

4. <h2>\(sweepHeading)</h2> — a <table> with columns: Check | Status | Note, listing every check run, including those that came back clear. Status is Clear, Exposed, Cannot verify on this record, or Not applicable — and a Not applicable row carries a one-line reason for why this forum or this instrument does not attract the check. The advocate must be able to see what was tested and cleared, not only what failed.

5. <h2>WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW</h2> — the documents you needed and did not have, the pages you could not read, the assertions nothing on this record supports, and every conditional finding with its test. Be specific about what to send for. "The record does not contain X" is a valuable output here; a plausible invented fact in its place is a liability.

6. <h2>\(actionHeading)</h2> — a numbered list in the order the work should be done, each item tied to the finding numbers it disposes of, separating what must be repaired now from what can be held back and dealt with \(isContract ? "in the next round of the mark-up" : "in reply or in argument").

IF A LENGTH LIMIT IS IMPOSED ON THIS RESPONSE, CUT IN THIS ORDER: keep the COVERAGE line, Section 1, the VERDICT and every Fatal and Serious finding in full, each with its pin-cite and its FIX; compress the sweep table to the Exposed and Cannot-verify rows only; reduce Material findings to their one line in the ranked table; drop the Minor findings and state how many were dropped. Never strip the Where / Why it hurts / How they will use it / FIX lines out of a Fatal or Serious finding to save space — those four lines are the entire product.

THIS OVERRIDES ANY GENERAL DRAFTING GUIDANCE APPENDED AFTER THIS PROMPT: in this tool a gap in the record is itself a finding and belongs in WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW. Do not fill a gap with a reasonable professional assumption and do not fill it with a [placeholder] — never a placeholder page number, party name, date, figure or citation. Bracketed assumptions are written for drafting a document; this is not a draft, and here an assumed fact reads as a found one and misleads the advocate about their own case. The only place a [bracket] belongs is inside FIX wording that the advocate will complete themselves.

IF THERE IS NO RECORD: if nothing has been attached and nothing pasted below, do not invent a draft and do not write as though you had read one. Say so in the COVERAGE line, then produce the pre-mortem for this \(isContract ? "instrument" : "document") type instead — \(noRecordFallback) — and head it plainly as a general checklist rather than as findings about their document. If only part of the record is present (the pleading but not its annexures, the agreement but not its schedules), work on what is there and list the rest under WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW rather than assuming what it contains.

MATERIAL UNDER REVIEW:
\(material)
\(worryNote)
"""
}

// MARK: - Highlighter

/// Highlighter — a numbers-and-terms extraction, not a summary.
///
/// Ported from the platform's `highlighter` tool definition. Its framing: the reader already knows
/// broadly what the document says; what they cannot do is read 200 pages to find the four figures
/// that decide the matter.
///
/// ## Why the scope dropdown maps to headings
///
/// From the source — *"A scope select that does not bind to a named output section is decoration:
/// neither the user nor the model can predict what narrowing actually produces."* Each option maps
/// to the exact heading the prompt defines, and the prompt is told to produce that one table and
/// expressly name what it omitted. A scope the user types himself falls through
/// ``SCOPE_TO_CATEGORY`` and is handled as free text against the same rules.
private let EVERYTHING = "Key Numbers & Critical Terms (everything material)"

/// An ordered map: Swift's `Dictionary` does not preserve insertion order and the dropdown's
/// option order is part of the port.
private let SCOPE_TO_CATEGORY: [(scope: String, category: String)] = [
    ("Money — Consideration, Payments & Schedule", "CONSIDERATION & PAYMENT"),
    ("Interest, Penalty & Liquidated Damages", "INTEREST, PENALTY & LIQUIDATED DAMAGES"),
    ("Caps, Indemnity Limits & Carve-outs", "CAPS, LIMITS & CARVE-OUTS"),
    ("Security — Deposit, EMD, Bank Guarantee & Retention", "SECURITY & CREDIT SUPPORT"),
    ("Time — Notice, Lock-in, Survival & Deadlines", "TIME, NOTICE & DEADLINES"),
    ("Termination, Default, Cure & Conditions Precedent", "TERMINATION, DEFAULT & CONDITIONS PRECEDENT"),
    ("Tax, GST, Stamp Duty & Registration", "TAX, STAMP & REGISTRATION"),
    ("Governing Law, Seat, Venue & Jurisdiction", "FORUM, LAW & DISPUTE CLAUSE"),
    ("Defined Terms Carrying a Number or a Condition", "DEFINED TERMS WITH TEETH"),
]

private func scopeCategory(_ scope: String) -> String? {
    SCOPE_TO_CATEGORY.first { $0.scope == scope }?.category
}

let HighlighterTool = ToolSpec(
    id: "highlighter",
    title: "Highlighter",
    short: "Numbers and hard terms",
    blurb: "Pull every material number, period, cap, rate and hard term out of a document — "
        + "exactly as written, pinned to where it appears.",
    output: .document,
    inputs: [
        ToolField(
            key: "extract",
            label: "What to extract",
            type: .select,
            options: [EVERYTHING] + SCOPE_TO_CATEGORY.map(\.scope)
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "party",
            label: "Reading for",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "e.g. the Buyer — marks each term as exposure or entitlement"
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "hunt",
            label: "Hunt specifically for",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "e.g. the liability cap, the lock-in period"
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "source",
            label: "Paste the text (or attach it above)",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "Paste the instrument… (leave blank if you have attached it)",
            big: true
        ),
    ],
    build: buildHighlighter
)

func buildHighlighter(_ v: ToolValues, _ today: Date) -> String {
    let chosen = v.text("extract", EVERYTHING)
    let scoped = chosen != EVERYTHING
    // A typed-in scope has no mapped heading; head the single table with the user's own words.
    let heading = scopeCategory(chosen) ?? chosen.uppercased()
    let partyCol = v.has("party") ? " | Effect on \(v.text("party", ""))" : ""

    let structure = scoped
        ? """
Then produce EXACTLY ONE category table, under the heading <h2>\(heading)</h2>, drawn from the category checklist below\(scopeCategory(chosen) != nil ? "" : " — and where the request answers to one of the categories below, treat that category's checklist as the floor and go wider if the request is wider"). Go through that category exhaustively: every value of that kind in the instrument, not a representative sample, in a table with the columns: Item | Value exactly as written | Trigger or condition | Where (document, page, clause)\(partyCol). Do NOT produce the other category tables. Immediately after that table, in one line, name the categories you have not produced on this run so the reader knows exactly what is missing and can re-run with "\(EVERYTHING)" to get them.
"""
        : """
Then one <h2> per category below, in the order given, each followed by a table with the columns: Item | Value exactly as written | Trigger or condition | Where (document, page, clause)\(partyCol). One row per extracted value. If a category genuinely has nothing in it, keep the heading and write "Nothing of this kind in the instrument" under it — a deleted heading is indistinguishable from a category you forgot to check.
"""

    let scopedNote = scoped
        ? " — this run is narrowed to that category. Go exhaustively through it, and still produce the SCANNED, AT A GLANCE, DISCREPANCIES and NOT IN THE RECORD sections, which apply always."
        : ""

    let partyBlock = v.has("party") ? """
READING FOR: \(v.text("party", "")). For every cap, penalty, interest charge, indemnity, deposit and notice obligation, mark whether it runs AGAINST this party (exposure) or IN ITS FAVOUR (entitlement); where it cuts both ways, say so.

""" : ""

    let huntBlock = v.has("hunt") ? """
HUNT SPECIFICALLY FOR: \(v.text("hunt", "")). If it is in the material, it is the FIRST ROW of AT A GLANCE, and it also gets its own full row, in full detail, in whichever category table it belongs to. If it genuinely is not there, say so in terms in NOT IN THE RECORD rather than offering the nearest thing.

""" : ""

    let sourceBlock = v.has("source") ? """

TEXT SUPPLIED BY THE USER:
\(v.text("source", ""))

""" : ""

    return """
Run a NUMBERS-AND-TERMS EXTRACTION over the material supplied. This is extraction, NOT a summary — the reader already knows broadly what the document says; what they cannot do is read 200 pages to find the four figures that decide the matter. Your whole value is that every material number, period, cap, rate and hard term is captured EXACTLY as written and pinned to the place it appears. Do not write a narrative summary of the document at any point.

The unit is the INSTRUMENT — the agreement, deed, notice, award or order in front of you, read together with its own schedules, annexures, amendments and side letters. An inconsistency here means an inconsistency INSIDE that family of papers; reading a set of separate documents against each other is a different tool (Doc Index), so do not turn this into a cross-document reconciliation.

EXTRACT: \(chosen)\(scopedNote)
\(partyBlock)\(huntBlock)\(sourceBlock)
FOUR RULES THAT GOVERN EVERY LINE YOU WRITE

1. REPRODUCE FIGURES EXACTLY. Copy each amount character-for-character as the document writes it — the Rs. / INR / ₹ marker, the digit grouping, the "/-", and the words-in-brackets form in full: "Rs. 1,50,00,000/- (Rupees One Crore Fifty Lakh only)", never "Rs. 15 million", "1.5 cr" or a re-grouped "Rs. 150,00,000". Indian grouping takes the last three digits together and then runs in twos — 1,50,00,000 reads 1 | 50 | 00 | 000, one crore fifty lakh. Read with Western three-digit grouping the same digit string comes out as 1,500,000, fifteen lakh, a tenth of the real figure, and that is the misread to guard against: count the commas from the right deliberately before you rely on any eight- or nine-digit amount. Never convert, round, normalise or abbreviate, and never drop the words in brackets. Carry the unit and the period with every figure — per annum, per month, per sq. ft., per incident, in the aggregate — because a rate without its period is a wrong number.
   - FOREIGN CURRENCY: reproduce a USD, EUR, GBP, AED or other foreign amount exactly as written, with its currency marker. Where the same obligation is expressed in both a foreign currency and rupees, capture BOTH figures, the conversion mechanic (the rate, its source — RBI reference rate, SBI TT selling rate, an agreed fixed rate — and the reference date on which it is struck), and where a cap or price is "the lower of" or "the higher of" the two, quote that formula verbatim and say which limb governs if the document itself fixes it. That formula is frequently the number the whole matter turns on.
   - NON-ROMAN SCRIPT: an amount written in Devanagari or a State language — common on stamp endorsements, receipts and older deeds — is reproduced as it appears, with the transliteration beside it marked "(transliterated)".
   - "(COMPUTED)": any figure you derive rather than read is marked "(computed)" immediately after it, with the inputs and the clause the formula comes from — an outside date worked out from a start point and a period, a cap expressed as a multiple of fees or as fees paid in the preceding twelve months, a percentage applied to the consideration, a total of instalments. An unmarked figure in this extract carries one meaning only: those characters appear on the page cited. Never let a derived number pass as a quoted one.
   - If a digit is illegible or the page is a poor scan, give your best reading followed by [figure unclear in source]; never a clean guess.

2. PIN-CITE EVERY VALUE. No number appears anywhere without its source beside it, in the form: document file name, page, and clause / paragraph / schedule / annexure / exhibit — "Facility Agreement.pdf, p. 27, cl. 8.3(b)" or "Lease Deed.pdf, p. 4, Sch. II". Say when the value sits in a schedule, annexure, definitions clause, recital, rate table or side letter rather than the operative clause, because that is usually where the real number hides. If you cannot establish the page, cite the clause and write "page not determinable" — do NOT invent a page number, a clause number or a document name. Where the meaning turns on the wording, quote the operative words verbatim beside the figure ("shall not exceed, in the aggregate" is a different animal from "shall not exceed, per claim").

3. SWEEP THE WHOLE INSTRUMENT. Do not extract only from the passage in front of you; work front to back through every page, including the definitions clause, all schedules, annexures and appendices, the payment and rate tables, the signature page, the stamp endorsement, and any addendum, amendment or side letter. On the pages you read, hunt the markers: every currency symbol, every percentage, every occurrence of lakh or crore, every count of days / months / years, and every phrase of the "shall not exceed / limited to / capped at / whichever is higher / whichever is earlier / within ___ days of" family. Where an amendment restates a figure, extract both the original and the amended figure and state which prevails. Where the record is too long to read end to end, follow the block below rather than quietly sampling it.

4. ABSENCE IS A FINDING. Where an expected term simply is not there, record that. Do not supply a market-standard figure and do not put a [placeholder] in a value column — a placeholder there is indistinguishable from a fabricated number. Write "Not stated in the record" and, for the material ones, add the consequence in one line (no liability cap means uncapped exposure; no clause on interest for delayed payment throws the claim back on statute; an arbitration clause naming no seat is a live problem).

WHERE THE RECORD IS LONGER THAN YOU CAN READ IN FULL
This tool exists for the long record, so a partial sweep is the normal case, not the failure case. The failure is a partial sweep presented as a complete one. Therefore:
- Call get_document_map on each attached file FIRST and work from its layout rather than reading from page 1 until you run out of room.
- Read, in this priority: the definitions clause; the consideration, payment and interest clauses; every schedule, annexure and rate table (a rate table is read whole or not at all); limitation of liability and indemnity; security and guarantee; termination and default; tax, stamp and registration including the endorsement; the dispute-resolution clause; the signature page; and every amendment, addendum and side letter.
- To locate what the map does not label, use search_knowledge_base — it is a MEANING-based search, so ask it in plain prose ("the limit on liability and the exceptions to that limit", "the payment schedule and the amount payable at each milestone", "interest payable on delayed payment"). Do not fire bare markers like "Rs." or "%" at it; those are what you look for with your eyes once the pages are in front of you. Then read_raw_pages over the pages it returns, plus a page either side, because a figure severed from its proviso is a wrong figure.
- Then say what you did not read. In SCANNED, give the page ranges actually read AND the page ranges NOT read, by number ("pp. 412-1,860 not read"). For an extraction tool a stated gap costs the reader nothing and a silent one costs him the case.

OUTPUT STRUCTURE — a heading, then tables, in this order

SCANNED: one line per document — its file name, the page ranges you actually read, and the page ranges you did not read (or "read in full" where you read all of it). Name any part you could not read because it is illegible, missing or truncated. Do not describe a page you have not read.

AT A GLANCE: a short table of the five to eight figures that actually decide this matter, most material first — Term | Value as written | Where. Nothing else in this table.

\(structure)

- CONSIDERATION & PAYMENT: price, fee, rent, salary or contract value; the payment schedule milestone by milestone with each due date; advance and mobilisation amounts; invoicing and credit period; escalation with its index, percentage and frequency; minimum guarantee, take-or-pay and price-revision mechanics.
- INTEREST, PENALTY & LIQUIDATED DAMAGES: the rate for delayed payment, whether simple or compounded and at what rests, penal or default interest, LD rate per day or week and its ceiling, forfeiture, and every "whichever is higher" formula written out. Where the payee is a registered micro or small enterprise supplier, add one line that the contractual rate may not be the rate that governs: ss. 15-16 of the MSMED Act, 2006 fix an outer limit of 45 days for payment and compound interest with monthly rests at three times the bank rate notified by the RBI, and that overrides the agreement.
- CAPS, LIMITS & CARVE-OUTS: the aggregate liability cap and how it is measured (a fixed sum, a multiple of fees, fees paid in the preceding twelve months); per-claim sub-caps; de minimis threshold and basket, and whether the basket tips or applies only to the excess; indemnity limits and their survival period; the EXPRESS CARVE-OUTS FROM THE CAP — fraud, wilful misconduct, breach of confidentiality, IP infringement, data protection, third-party indemnity, payment obligations — listed individually even though they carry no figure, because they are the heads of unlimited liability; and the exclusion of indirect or consequential loss, noting specifically whether loss of profit is excluded.
- SECURITY & CREDIT SUPPORT: security deposit and its refund mechanics, EMD or bid security, performance bank guarantee with amount, validity and claim period, retention money and its release milestones, advance BG, corporate or personal guarantee and the amount guaranteed, and any mortgage, hypothecation or pledge with the secured amount.
- TIME, NOTICE & DEADLINES: term and commencement date, lock-in, renewal or extension window, the termination notice period for EACH party separately (they are frequently different, and that difference matters), exclusivity or standstill, the claim-notification and survival windows, force-majeure notice, and time-is-of-the-essence wording. Where a clause fixes both a start point and a period, give the computed outside date, marked "(computed)" under Rule 1. Where a clause shortens the limitation period, or extinguishes a right or discharges a party once a contractual window expires, flag it against s. 28 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872 — an internationally drafted agreement carries such a clause as a matter of course and in India it is void, subject to the exception for a guarantee in favour of a bank or financial institution where the specified period is not less than one year.
- TERMINATION, DEFAULT & CONDITIONS PRECEDENT: every termination trigger, one row each — termination for convenience, material breach, payment default, insolvency or the admission of a s. 7 / s. 9 application under the IBC, 2016, change of control, cross-default with its threshold figure, a material adverse change and how MAC is defined, and any regulatory or licence trigger. For each: which party may terminate on it, the notice required, the cure period and whether the cure period runs from the breach or from the notice, and the consequence on termination (refund, forfeiture, exit payment, transition obligation). Then the conditions precedent, listed individually with who bears each and its deadline, the long-stop date and what happens if it passes, and any condition subsequent.
- TAX, STAMP & REGISTRATION: GST rate, who bears it and whether the price is inclusive or exclusive, reverse charge, TDS rate with the section and any gross-up obligation, the stamp duty recited against the stamp actually affixed and the State whose stamp law applies (flag any shortfall or missing endorsement — an insufficiently stamped instrument is liable to be impounded and is inadmissible until duty and penalty are paid, under s. 35 of the Indian Stamp Act, 1899 or the corresponding provision of the applicable State Stamp Act, such as ss. 33-34 of the Maharashtra Stamp Act, 1958; cite the provision of the State whose law governs, not the Central Act by default), and registration where the instrument requires it (s. 17 of the Registration Act, 1908, a lease for a term exceeding one year being the common trap — and give the consequence under s. 49: the unregistered instrument does not affect the immovable property and is not receivable as evidence of the transaction, save for a collateral purpose).
- FORUM, LAW & DISPUTE CLAUSE: governing law; seat of arbitration; venue; institution and rules; number of arbitrators and the appointment mechanism; language; the jurisdiction clause and whether it is exclusive; pre-arbitration steps with their time limits; and any monetary threshold that routes a dispute to a different track. Flag squarely where seat and venue are named inconsistently, or where only a "venue" is named and no seat, since the seat fixes the court with supervisory jurisdiction.
- DEFINED TERMS WITH TEETH: every defined term whose definition itself carries a number, a period, a formula or a condition — the operative clause reads harmlessly until the definition is read against it. Give the definition verbatim and the clause it bites in.

DISCREPANCIES & INTERNAL INCONSISTENCIES: this is often the single most valuable thing this extract produces, so run it deliberately rather than in passing. Compare each figure against every other appearance of the same figure and report: (a) numerals that do not match the amount written in words in the same clause — "Rs. 1,50,00,000/- (Rupees Fifteen Lakh only)" — reported even where the intended figure seems obvious; on a negotiable instrument (cheque, promissory note, bill of exchange) s. 18 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 settles it in favour of the amount in words, but in an ordinary commercial contract s. 18 has no application, so give both versions and flag the conflict for construction rather than declaring a winner; (b) the same amount, rate, period or date stated differently in two places (operative clause against schedule, recital, annexure or rate table); (c) arithmetic that does not close — instalments that do not total the consideration, percentages that do not reach 100, schedule line items that exceed the cap; (d) cross-references to a clause, schedule or annexure that is missing, misnumbered, or does not say what the reference claims; (e) periods that cannot both be satisfied, such as a cure period longer than the notice that triggers it. Table it as: What conflicts | Version A with pin-cite | Version B with pin-cite | Why it matters.
Not everything that looks like a conflict is one, and a false discrepancy in this table destroys the reader's trust in the true ones. The same amount restated in another currency, or in lakh or crore beside the numeral, or in words beside the figures, is a restatement and not a discrepancy; report it only where the two do not reconcile on the document's own conversion rate and reference date, or where the document supplies no rate or date at all for a conversion that governs a payment.

NOT IN THE RECORD: list the material terms you looked for and did not find, each with the consequence of its absence in one line. Where a term may sit in a page range you did not read, say that instead of saying it is absent. Do not pad this with terms the instrument would never contain.

If nothing has been attached and no text pasted, say so in a single line and set out the checklist of what will be extracted once the document is supplied — do not invent a specimen document, and do not produce example figures. If the material supplied is only an extract of a longer instrument, say which parts you could not see.

Render each section as an <h2> followed by a <table> with a header row, values in <tbody> rows. Keep the value column and the pin-cite column as separate columns in every table, so a figure can never appear without its source beside it. If the length instruction requires brevity, keep every category and cut WITHIN each one by materiality — the highest-value rows only, and a count of what was left out under that table ("9 further payment-milestone rows omitted") — because dropping a whole category can cost the reader the one number that decides the matter. Never drop the pin-cite column and never truncate or abbreviate a figure. Every row must be verifiable in under a minute by turning to the page you cite.
"""
}

// MARK: - Doc Index

/// Doc Index — read a set of documents against each other.
///
/// Ported from the platform's `docIndex` tool definition. The product is not a summary of each
/// document but the differences between them: the same particular stated two ways in two places is
/// the finding, and ``RECORD_FOCUS`` is what tells the model where to look for it in each kind of
/// record.
private let RECORD_FOCUS_GENERIC =
    """
Reconcile every particular that appears in more than one document — names, addresses, dates, amounts, quantities, reference and account numbers — and treat the differences between them as the product.
"""

/// An ordered map, for the same reason ``SCOPE_TO_CATEGORY`` is one: these keys are the "Kind of
/// record" dropdown, in this order.
private let RECORD_FOCUS: [(kind: String, focus: String)] = [
    (
        "Litigation brief / paperbook",
        """
Read the pleaded case against the documents: where a pleading, affidavit or written statement asserts a fact that no exhibit on the file supports, say so. Contradictions between a deposition or a prior statement and a contemporaneous document are cross-examination material — mark those entries "for cross" and set out the confrontation in one line (the prior statement, the document to be put, the question).
"""
    ),
    (
        "Due diligence data room",
        """
Read the disclosures, schedules and representations against the underlying instruments: a warranty or disclosure the instrument does not bear out is the finding. Quantify each discrepancy in rupees (lakh / crore) wherever the documents permit it, and say whether it goes to price, to an indemnity, or to a condition precedent.
"""
    ),
    (
        "Agreement + amendments & annexures",
        """
Track each material term — consideration, term and renewal, termination and notice period, exclusivity, liability cap, indemnity, governing law and seat — across the original agreement, every amendment, side letter, purchase order and annexure. For each term state the operative version as it now stands, the instrument that fixed it and its date, and flag any term varied by a document that does not properly recite the variation or that is not signed by both sides.
"""
    ),
    (
        "Correspondence & notices",
        """
Match every notice, demand and reminder to its reply. Flag unanswered notices, replies that have no antecedent letter on the file, and any notice with no proof of dispatch or service. Where a date engages limitation, an acknowledgement of liability, or a contractual cure period, say so with the date and the pin-cite.
"""
    ),
    (
        "Invoices, accounts & payment records",
        """
Reconcile the money trail end to end: purchase order to invoice to delivery challan / GRN to payment instrument to ledger entry to statement of account. Show every figure that does not carry through, giving both figures with their pin-cites, and total the unexplained difference in rupees.
"""
    ),
    (
        "Title deeds & property chain",
        """
Set out the devolution of title in order — each transfer, its instrument, date and registration particulars — and identify every break in the chain. Compare the schedule of the property across the deeds: extent or area, survey / khasra number, boundaries and abutting owners. Flag encumbrances, instruments that are unregistered or insufficiently stamped, and any deed that conveys more than its predecessor held.
"""
    ),
    (
        "Company & regulatory filings",
        """
Reconcile the resolutions and minutes against what was actually filed with the Registrar and against the statutory registers: dates of appointment, resignation and cessation, allotments and share capital, creation and satisfaction of charges, and the figures carried into the financial statements. A filing that does not match the minute behind it is the finding.
"""
    ),
    (
        "FIR, statements & investigation papers",
        """
Compare the FIR, the statements recorded under s.180 BNSS (old s.161 CrPC), the panchnamas and seizure memos, the medical and forensic reports and the charge-sheet against one another: timings, sequence, place, the recovery and its witnesses, and the description each witness gives. Omissions and improvements between the earliest version and the latest are the material to isolate. Frame every confrontation within the law that governs it, or it will be disallowed the moment it is put: a statement to the police may be used ONLY to contradict its maker and only in the manner of s.148 BSA (old s.145 Evidence Act), the witness first having his attention drawn to the specific part to be used; s.181 BNSS (old s.162 CrPC) bars its use for any other purpose, including corroboration. An omission is a contradiction only where it is significant and otherwise relevant, the test being Tahsildar Singh — a mere absence of detail is not a contradiction, so never present one as though it were.
"""
    ),
]

private func recordFocus(_ kind: String?) -> String? {
    guard let kind else { return nil }
    return RECORD_FOCUS.first { $0.kind == kind }?.focus
}

let DocIndexTool = ToolSpec(
    id: "doc-index",
    title: "Doc Index",
    short: "Cross-check the set",
    blurb: "Index a set of related documents and read them against each other — "
        + "contradictions, missing annexures, and what the record actually proves.",
    output: .document,
    inputs: [
        ToolField(
            key: "deliverable",
            label: "What do you need",
            type: .select,
            options: [
                "Full cross-document review",
                "Index of the record",
                "Contradictions & discrepancies",
                "Documents referred to but not produced",
                "What the record proves vs. what is unproven",
            ]
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "recordType",
            label: "Kind of record",
            type: .select,
            // Every option is a key of RECORD_FOCUS, which is what makes the choice change the
            // instructions rather than just the heading.
            options: RECORD_FOCUS.map(\.kind)
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "matter",
            label: "Matter / transaction",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "e.g. Sterling Infra Pvt. Ltd. v. NHAI — arbitration record (optional)"
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "focus",
            label: "Particulars to cross-check",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "Name the facts to reconcile across the set — the principal amount, "
                + "the date of termination, the consignment quantity, a witness whose versions "
                + "differ… (optional)",
            big: true
        ),
        // A paste field, not decoration: without it the cross-check has nothing to cross-check
        // when the user has the text but not the files.
        ToolField(
            key: "source",
            label: "Or paste the documents",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "Paste the text of two or more documents here if you have not "
                + "attached them — keep whatever separates them so they can be told apart…",
            big: true
        ),
    ],
    build: buildDocIndex
)

func buildDocIndex(_ v: ToolValues, _ today: Date) -> String {
    let focusBlock = v.has("focus") ? """
Cross-check these particulars in priority to everything else: \(v.text("focus", ""))

""" : ""

    let sourceBlock = v.has("source") ? """

DOCUMENTS PASTED BY THE USER — treat this text as part of the record. Where it holds more than one document, split it at its boundaries exactly as above, D-number each one and read them against each other and against anything attached:
\(v.text("source", ""))

""" : ""

    return """
You are reviewing a SET of related documents together. The unit of analysis is the SET, never any one document in it. The product is the work that only emerges from reading these documents against each other — what two of them say differently, what one of them refers to that is not on the file, and what the set collectively proves. Do not produce a document-by-document summary; a summary of a single document is a different tool.

WHAT COUNTS AS A DOCUMENT — settle this before you decide there is little to do
The unit is the DOCUMENT, not the attachment. Indian records ordinarily arrive as ONE file holding many documents: a scanned paperbook, a compiled annexure bundle, a convenience compilation, an exported email or messaging chain, a run of bank statements, a charge-sheet with its annexures. Such a file IS a set, and usually the richest one you will get. Before anything else, split it at its internal boundaries — its own index or list of documents, exhibit and annexure markings, changes of letterhead, date lines and subscriptions, signature and attestation blocks, stamp and registration endorsements, From / To blocks, and restarts of internal pagination — and treat each constituent as its own D-numbered item. Pin-cite to the page within that file, because that is the page the reader will turn to. Only a record that is genuinely one instrument, with nothing else bound with it, takes the single-instrument branch at the end.

Matter / transaction: \(v.text("matter", "[as identified from the attached record]"))
Test every document in the set against that matter. A paperbook and a data room both routinely carry paper from another transaction, another party or another year, picked up when the file was compiled. Where a document does not belong to this matter, say so in its index row and again in section 1 — a stray document is a set-level finding no single-document reading can make, and it is sometimes the finding that matters, because it shows what else the compiler had on his desk.
Kind of record: \(v.text("recordType", "Mixed litigation / transaction record"))
Deliverable: \(v.text("deliverable", "Full cross-document review"))
\(focusBlock)\(sourceBlock)
HOW TO READ THIS SET: \(recordFocus(v.raw("recordType")) ?? RECORD_FOCUS_GENERIC)

SWEEP THE WHOLE RECORD BEFORE YOU CONCLUDE ANYTHING
Work front to back through every document in the set rather than reasoning from whatever passages come to hand. Read specific page ranges and search the record by name, figure and date for the particulars you expect to exist and have not yet located. For each recurring particular — the parties' names, the addresses, the contract / account / registration number, the principal sum, the rate, the material dates, quantities, area and survey numbers — find EVERY place it appears before you call it consistent. A particular you did not go looking for is not a particular that agrees. Then record the extent of the sweep in the SCANNED line below and let that line, not an impression, govern what you are entitled to say.

CITATION DISCIPLINE — this is what makes the output usable at a desk:
- Number every document D1, D2, D3 ... in the index below and use those numbers everywhere afterwards. Number them in the order the record itself carries where it has one — paperbook pagination, exhibit and annexure marking, the data-room index — because that is the order of the file in the user's hands; only where the record has no order of its own, number by date.
- Keep the record's own citation key (Ex. P-2, Annexure A-4, the paperbook page range) in its own column, never buried in prose, so it can be read down the page and matched to the file.
- Every factual assertion carries a pin-cite in square brackets: [D3 p.12 para 4], [D3, Ex. P-2, cl. 7.1], [D5, Annexure A-4]. Where the document sits inside a larger file, cite the page of that file.
- Where a discrepancy turns on the exact words, open that page, read it, and quote the words verbatim. Never report a discrepancy you cannot pin-cite on BOTH sides — drop it instead.
- If the text of a page is illegible, truncated or missing from what you can read, identify the document and page and say so. Do not guess at what it said.

FIGURES AND DATES MUST BE READ OFF THE PAGE, NOT RECALLED
This tool's product is the comparison of figures and dates across documents, and a scanned Indian record is exactly where that goes wrong. Before any figure, date or reference number is put forward as a difference, go back to the page it came from and confirm the reading there.
- Indian digit grouping runs 2-2-3 from the right, so Rs. 1,50,00,000 is one crore fifty lakh, not fifteen million. Read the grouping deliberately and reproduce the figure character for character, including the words in brackets where the document gives them; where the document states the amount in words as well, use the words to confirm the numerals.
- Scanning routinely mangles 5 and 6, 1 and 7, 3 and 8, 0 and the letter o, and drops or moves the commas that carry the lakh-crore grouping. A difference between two documents that disappears once the words or the original are read is an artefact, not a contradiction.
- Where the source is a poor scan or a digit is not certain, give your best reading followed by [reading unverified — check the original]. Such a value may NOT be asserted as a contradiction anywhere in this output: it goes into the register in section 4 with the status "Unverified" and a line saying which page must be checked against the original. A fabricated contradiction that collapses when the original is produced costs the user more than a missed one.

Structure the output with a heading per section below, in this order, using tables where a table is specified.

SCANNED
One line per document actually read: its D-number, the file it came from (with the pages within that file where several documents share one), and the page range you in fact read. Then one line stating whether coverage is complete, and naming every document or page range not fully read. Never estimate this line and never omit it — it is what tells the reader whether the record was swept or sampled.

1. AT A GLANCE
At most five lines. The most material findings in the whole set — a contradiction, a missing document, an admission, a document belonging to another matter — each in one sentence with both pin-cites. Write "No material discrepancy found across the set" ONLY where the SCANNED line records complete coverage of every document. Where coverage is not complete, the finding is "No discrepancy found in the pages read", followed by what remains unread. Do not manufacture a finding to fill the section, and do not issue a clean bill of health over a record you have only sampled.

2. INDEX OF THE RECORD
A table, one row per document. Columns: No. (D1, D2 ...) | Record marking — the exhibit or annexure mark, or the paperbook / file page range it occupies | Document — its own title if it bears one, and the file it came from | Date on the document (write "undated" where it bears none; never substitute a file date) | Parties — author to recipient, or the parties to the instrument | What it establishes, in one sentence.
Where the numbering is by date, an undated document sits immediately after the last document that can be dated from the surrounding record, marked "undated — placed by position in the file"; never invent a date to sort it.
Below the table, in one line: the number of documents, the date span from earliest to latest, any document that cannot be dated or attributed from its own face, and any document that does not belong to the matter named above.

3. CONTRADICTIONS BETWEEN DOCUMENTS
The highest-value section. A table ordered by materiality, money and dates first. Columns: Fact in issue | What one document says — verbatim words with pin-cite | What the other says — verbatim words with pin-cite | Why it matters and what it is worth.
Enter a row here only where the two statements cannot both be true AND both readings have been confirmed against their pages. In the last column, for a litigation record say how the discrepancy would be put to a witness or to the other side; for a transaction record say the rupee, warranty or closing consequence.
A particular recorded two ways that can still both stand — a name spelled or transliterated differently, an address variant, rounding, a different date format — does NOT belong in this table. It is registered in section 4 as "Variant". Keep it out so that this table stays trustworthy, but never drop it there: a wrong name or a superseded address on a demand notice can defeat service and with it the notice.
Every row in this table also appears in the section 4 register marked "Contradiction". Use the same particular name in both so the reader never has to reconcile two tables to be sure nothing is in only one of them.

4. RECURRING PARTICULARS — THE REGISTER
The single register of every particular that appears in more than one document, so that one table answers what the record says about each: the parties' full legal names and constitution, addresses, the contract / policy / account / loan / registration number, the principal sum, the rate of interest, the dates of execution, delivery, payment, breach and termination, quantities, and area or survey numbers. Columns: Particular | Every distinct value found, each followed by the documents that state it — for example "Rs. 1,20,00,000 [D2 p.3, D5 p.1]; Rs. 1,02,00,000 [D7 p.9]" | Status | Note.
Status takes one of four values and nothing else:
- Consistent — the same in every place it appears, having actually looked in every place.
- Variant — recorded differently but both versions can stand. In the Note say where the variance can still bite: service of a notice, identification of the property, matching a payment to an invoice.
- Contradiction — the versions cannot both be true. These, and only these, are carried up into section 3.
- Unverified — the difference rests on a poor scan or an uncertain digit. Name the page to be checked against the original. An Unverified row is never reported as a contradiction.

5. REFERRED TO BUT NOT PRODUCED
Every document the record itself mentions that is absent from the set: an annexure or exhibit named but not filed, an earlier agreement recited in a later deed, a letter that a reply on the file answers, a board approval a form refers to, invoices a ledger relies on, proof of dispatch or service for a notice. A table with columns: Document referred to | Where it is referred to (pin-cite) | What it would establish | How it is obtained and what its absence costs.
In that last column be specific and be practical: the client, a public office (certified copy from the Sub-Registrar, an ROC / MCA search, RTI), or the opposite party. Where the document is one referred to in a pleading or affidavit, that is a notice to produce under Order XI r.15 CPC. Where it is a document the plaintiff himself relies on but did not file with the plaint, add the consequence that makes this worth raising: under Order VII r.14(3) CPC it shall not be received in evidence at the hearing without the leave of the Hon'ble Court, so the omission is a live objection and not merely an observation. If the suit is a commercial dispute, say so and note that Order XI CPC stands substituted by the Commercial Courts Act, 2015 and disclosure and inspection run on that substituted regime instead.
Then a short list headed "Record integrity" of defects on the face of the documents actually produced: unsigned, undated, unstamped or insufficiently stamped, unregistered where registration is compulsory, unattested where attestation is required, illegible or partial pages, a page sequence that jumps, and copies of copies without certification. Run one further check here on every electronic record in the set — email, messaging thread, SMS, screenshot, photograph, CCTV or camera footage, server or call-data log, computer-generated ledger or bank statement, portal download, e-way bill: state whether a certificate under s.63(4) BSA (old s.65B Evidence Act) has been produced with it. Run this check on the electronic records themselves whether or not any document on the file mentions a certificate, because its absence is a defect in the mode of proof of what HAS been produced, and on an email-heavy or screenshot-heavy record it is what decides admissibility.

6. WHERE THE CHAIN BREAKS
Not a chronology — the user has a separate tool that builds one, and duplicating it wastes the section. List only the documents that take part in a break or in a notice-and-reply pair, and state the break itself: a reply with no antecedent letter on the file, a notice with no reply and no proof of service, an unexplained silence between two related events, a term acted upon before the document that created it, a document dated after the event it purports to record, a payment with no invoice behind it. One line each, both pin-cited. Where a date in the record actually engages limitation, an acknowledgement under s.18 of the Limitation Act, 1963, or a contractual cure period, say so with the date; do not raise limitation where no date on the record engages it.

7. WHAT THE SET COLLECTIVELY ESTABLISHES
Two lists, both pin-cited.
(a) Established on this record: propositions carried by two or more independent documents, or by an admission in the other side's own document — name the documents that carry each, strongest first.
(b) Not established on this record: what is asserted or assumed but rests on a single unsupported document, an unsigned or undated paper, hearsay, or nothing at all. Write it plainly — "the record does not contain any document evidencing X" is a finding, and a more valuable one than a guess.

8. NEXT STEPS ON THE RECORD
At most six lines, each operational: which document to call for and from whom, which discrepancy to put to which witness or counterparty, which certified copy or public search to obtain to close a gap, which page to check against the original.

WHICH SECTIONS THE DELIVERABLE CALLS FOR
SCANNED and section 1 are produced whatever was asked for. Beyond them:
- "Full cross-document review" — all eight sections in order.
- "Index of the record" — section 2 alone, at full depth, followed by the "Record integrity" list from section 5. Nothing else.
- "Contradictions & discrepancies" — section 2 in short form (No., record marking, document, date, parties only, to fix the D-numbers), then sections 3 and 4 in full.
- "Documents referred to but not produced" — section 2 in short form, then section 5 in full including "Record integrity", then only those lines of section 8 that go to obtaining the missing material.
- "What the record proves vs. what is unproven" — section 2 in short form, then section 7 in full, and where a proposition fails for want of a document, name that document as section 5 would.
- Anything else the user names — follow the closest of the above.

IF THE LENGTH INSTRUCTION APPENDED BELOW REQUIRES BREVITY
Cut in this order and no other: section 6 first, then section 8, then the Note and commentary columns, then the number of particulars carried in section 4, most material retained. Never drop section 3, never drop the SCANNED coverage line, never drop a pin-cite, and never truncate, round or abbreviate a figure to save space.

IF THE RECORD IS GENUINELY A SINGLE INSTRUMENT
Take this branch only after satisfying yourself that the attachment is one document and not one file holding several — a paperbook, a bundle, a compilation or a correspondence chain is a set, however it arrived, and must be split and reviewed as one. Where the record truly is a single instrument, say so in the first line: this tool reads documents against one another and one document gives it almost nothing to do. Then give only (a) its index row, (b) the documents it itself refers to that are not produced, (c) any inconsistency internal to it, and (d) a short, specific list of the related documents that should be attached for the cross-check to be worth running — named from what that document refers to. Stop there. Do not pad the output with a summary of that one document.

IF NOTHING IS ATTACHED AND NOTHING PASTED
Do not invent a record: say in one line that no documents were received, then set out the checklist of documents that a record of this kind would ordinarily comprise so the user knows what to collect, and stop.

HONESTY RULES, ABOVE EVERYTHING
- Never state a date, amount, figure, number or name that does not appear on a page you can cite.
- Where two documents differ, report both versions; never silently prefer one, and never average or reconcile them yourself.
- Do not soften a genuine contradiction into "a possible inconsistency", and do not inflate a difference of wording into a contradiction.
- A gap in the record, stated plainly, is a result. A filled gap is a liability.
"""
}

// MARK: - List of Dates

/// List of Dates — a filing-ready chronology.
///
/// Ported from `emperor-ai/src/tools/definitions/listOfDates.js`. The prompt body below was
/// extracted from that file mechanically, not retyped: it runs to ~18,000 characters of procedural
/// checklist, and a dropped clause is a tool that quietly stops doing the thing it exists for.
///
/// The platform's own note explains why this is not just the general work-up tool: a List of Dates
/// sits at the front of the paperbook, the Bench reads it before the pleadings, and the other side
/// goes at it line by line — so it needs issued-vs-served precision, evidenced-vs-asserted tagging,
/// conflicting dates shown rather than resolved, and the limitation arithmetic.
let ListOfDatesTool = ToolSpec(
    id: "list-of-dates",
    title: "List of Dates",
    short: "Court-ready chronology",
    blurb: "Turn your record into a filing-ready Synopsis and List of Dates — every entry "
        + "pin-cited, conflicts flagged, limitation intervals computed.",
    // `document`, not `timeline`: this is filed rather than read on screen. The platform tried
    // the timeline renderer and reverted — it parses only a 4-digit year, so it discarded day
    // precision, the page column and the conflict flags.
    output: .document,
    inputs: [
        ToolField(
            key: "format",
            label: "What do you need",
            type: .select,
            options: [
                "Synopsis and List of Dates (for filing)",
                "List of Dates only (for filing)",
                "Working Chronology (internal)",
                "Limitation Chronology",
            ]
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "forum",
            label: "Court / forum",
            type: .select,
            options: FORUMS
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "party",
            label: "Prepared on behalf of",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "e.g. the Petitioner, Sterling Infra Pvt. Ltd."
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "focus",
            label: "Focus / instructions",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "An issue, transaction or period to treat in more depth — or paste "
                + "the facts here if you have not attached the record…",
            big: true
        ),
    ],
    build: buildListOfDates
)

func buildListOfDates(_ v: ToolValues, _ today: Date) -> String {
    let focusBlock = v.has("focus") ? """
Counsel's instructions / focus:
\(v.text("focus", ""))
This governs the DEPTH of treatment, not what goes in. Every dated event on the record stays in the table; the events counsel has pointed to get fuller Event and Note text and closer citation. Restrict the chronology to a period or a transaction only where counsel has expressly asked for that restriction, and where you do, say so on the face of the document ("Restricted chronology: events from DD.MM.YYYY to DD.MM.YYYY only") so nobody mistakes it for the full List.

""" : ""

    return """
Prepare a LIST OF DATES for an Indian litigation matter from the case record. This is not a summary. In Indian practice the List of Dates is a formal part of the paperbook: the Hon'ble Court reads it before it reads the pleadings, and the other side goes through it line by line. One wrong or unsupported date discredits the whole document. Precision outranks coverage, and an admitted gap outranks a confident guess.

Deliverable: \(v.text("format", "Synopsis and List of Dates (for filing)")).
Forum: \(v.text("forum", "as the record indicates")). Match that forum's convention — in the Supreme Court the Synopsis and List of Dates is required by the Supreme Court Rules, 2013 and the Practice Directions issued thereunder, and page references are to the paperbook; in the High Courts a synopsis and list of dates precedes a writ petition, but whether it is required at all and how pages are referred to differ between the Delhi, Bombay, Madras and other High Courts, so read the Court's name off the cause-title in the record and state which Court's convention you have assumed, so that counsel can correct it in one line instead of discovering it at the filing counter; before the NCLT, NCLAT and other tribunals the same chronology is filed alongside the statement on limitation.
Prepared on behalf of: \(v.text("party", "[the filing party, as identified from the record]")). Refer to the parties by their status in the present proceedings (Petitioner, Respondent, Appellant, Complainant), giving the full name at first mention. The narrative may be told from this party's standpoint, but never at the cost of an accurate date and never by dropping an event that is on the record.
\(focusBlock)
WHERE THE DATES MUST COME FROM
Go through the attached documents front to back rather than skimming; read specific page ranges where you need to and search the record for dates you expect to exist but have not yet located. Harvest dates from where they hide, not only from the narrative: the date line and subscription of each letter, postal and courier receipts, speed-post tracking, dishonour memos, endorsements, seals and stamps, acknowledgements, the attestation date on an affidavit, the vakalatnama, order sheets and daily proceedings, the date of pronouncement on a judgment, the registration endorsement on a deed, invoice and payment dates, and statutory filing acknowledgements.
If nothing has been attached and no facts appear in the instructions above, do not invent a matter: say plainly that no record was supplied, then output the correct blank court format (the table with its headings) with a checklist of the documents and the classes of dated events counsel should supply for a matter of this kind. Invent no party, no date and no case number.
If facts have been pasted instead of documents attached, build the chronology from those facts but mark every entry as resting on counsel's instructions and not yet verified against a document on record.

SAY WHAT YOU ACTUALLY READ
A List of Dates that is silently partial is worse than no List at all, because it still reads as complete and it still gets filed. A paperbook can run to thousands of pages and you may not be able to cover all of it. Therefore, in the notes block of a filed version and at the head of an internal chronology, state in plain terms: (a) the documents and the page ranges you actually read; (b) the documents that were attached but which you did not read, or could not read; and (c) where the record could not be covered end to end, that the List is PROVISIONAL and confined to the material covered, and what remains to be gone through. Never present partial coverage as complete, and never pad the table to make it look complete.

LENGTH
A preferred output length is appended to this request. It governs the SYNOPSIS and the notes only. It does not govern the table: no dated event on the record may be dropped from the table, and no entry may be compressed to the point where the act it records is no longer identifiable, in order to meet a length preference. Where the record is genuinely too large to list in full, say so, list what you covered and mark the List provisional — never truncate the table without notice.

THE SEVEN RULES THAT MAKE A LIST OF DATES LEGALLY CORRECT

1. NAME THE ACT, NOT THE DOCUMENT. Issue, despatch, service, receipt, refusal and reply are separate events with separate legal consequences, and limitation almost always runs from one specific one. Never collapse them into a single row to save space. Where the record shows more than one, each gets its own row and the Event column must state which act occurred:
   - notice issued / despatched / served / received / refused / returned unclaimed;
   - order reserved / pronounced / signed / uploaded, and certified copy applied for / prepared / delivered (those three decide the Section 12 Limitation Act exclusion);
   - suit or petition presented / registered / returned for compliance / re-filed;
   - cheque drawn / presented / dishonoured / memo received / statutory notice under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 despatched and served / fifteen-day period expired / complaint filed — these rows exist so that counsel can test the three statutory intervals set out under LIMITATION below, so do not merge them;
   - agreement executed / registered / possession delivered / consideration paid;
   - notice invoking arbitration under Section 21 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 issued and received / tribunal constituted / statement of claim filed / award made and received / petition under Section 34 filed.
   Where the record shows only one half of such a pair (a notice dated 05.03.2019 with no proof of service), say so in that row instead of assuming the other half happened.

2. SEPARATE WHAT IS EVIDENCED FROM WHAT IS MERELY ASSERTED. A date proved by a dated instrument on the record is a different animal from a date averred in a pleading. Classify every entry:
   D — evidenced by a document on the record (the dated instrument itself, an endorsement, a receipt, an order);
   P — asserted in a pleading, affidavit, notice or written submission only, with no supporting document on record;
   I — inferred, computed or approximate.
   This is precisely what opposing counsel will attack, so it must be visible to counsel even where it is not printed in the filed version.

3. NORMALISE THE DATE, PRESERVE WHAT THE DOCUMENT SAID. The same date appears in Indian documents as 05.03.2019, 5th March 2019, 05/03/2019, 5-3-19 and March 5, 2019. Render every date in the output as DD.MM.YYYY.
   - Where the document's own rendering differs materially from the normalised form — a spelled-out date, a two-digit year, a date written in words in a deed — record the source form in the notes ("the page reads 'the Fifth day of March, Two Thousand Nineteen'"), because opposing counsel will check the List against the page.
   - Where a numeric date is genuinely ambiguous between DD/MM and MM/DD (03/05/2019), do not silently pick a reading. Resolve it only from context — the surrounding sequence, a spelled-out date for the same event elsewhere, the document's own convention — and state the basis. If it cannot be resolved, keep the document's own form, mark it AMBIGUOUS and say which reading changes what.
   - Where the year is given in two digits (5-3-19), do not assume the century. Fix it only from the record — the age of the instrument, the stamp or registration particulars, the surrounding sequence — and say what fixed it. In succession, title and property matters 1919 and 2019 are both live; where the record cannot settle it, give both readings and mark it AMBIGUOUS.
   - Where only a month, quarter or financial year is given, render it as given (03.2019, FY 2019-20), place it after the last certain date within that period and mark "(day not stated)". Do not manufacture a day.
   - Compute relative expressions ("within two weeks thereafter", "the following Monday") only where the anchor date is certain, and show the computation in the entry; otherwise keep the words.
   - Where the document carries an obvious internal error, such as a letter dated after the reply it answers, reproduce what it says and flag the discrepancy. Do not correct it silently.

4. NEVER RECONSTRUCT A DIGIT. Indian paperbooks are scans of scans. Dates come through mangled by OCR (l5.l2.20l8, O5.O3.2Ol9, 0S.03.20I9), sit under a round stamp or a seal, are cut off at the margin, or are written in hand on an endorsement. The temptation is to repair the character into the plausible date — that is fabrication, and here it lands in the Date column of a document that goes to the Bench. Where a date cannot be read with confidence: reproduce the characters exactly as they appear, mark the entry ILLEGIBLE with the document name and page, state the readings the marks could bear if any are open, and name what must be done — the page to be re-scanned, the certified copy or the original to be obtained, the deponent to be asked. Do the same for a date obscured by a stamp and for a handwritten endorsement you cannot read. An ILLEGIBLE row that names the page is useful; a clean row carrying a guessed digit is a liability.

5. PIN-CITE EVERY ENTRY. No entry without a source. Cite the document by the name it carries in the record together with the page, paragraph or annexure — in a Supreme Court or High Court paperbook the form is "Annexure P-1 @ p. 45", "Impugned order dated 12.04.2021 @ p. 112", "Writ Petition, para 7"; in a trial-court record it is the exhibit form, "Ex. P-3, p. 45". Where the attached paperbook is paginated, use the paperbook page, because that is the page the Hon'ble Court will turn to; otherwise cite the document's own page. If you cannot cite an entry, do not write it.

6. CONFLICTS ARE OUTPUT, NOT NOISE. Where two sources give different dates for the same event, do not choose. Show the event once carrying both dates and both citations, mark it CONFLICT, and say which source is the stronger — a contemporaneous document ordinarily beats a later pleading — without suppressing the other.

7. SILENCE IS OUTPUT TOO. Where the record does not establish something, say so in terms ("the record does not show when the notice was served") and name the document that would establish it. A stated gap is useful to counsel; a filled gap is a liability. Never supply a date, figure, account number or party name that is not in the record.

STRUCTURE OF THE DOCUMENT
Order strictly by date; where two events share a date, order them as the record shows they occurred.

If the deliverable is "Synopsis and List of Dates (for filing)" or "List of Dates only (for filing)":
- Open with a single heading, SYNOPSIS AND LIST OF DATES (or LIST OF DATES), and under it one line identifying the matter to the extent the record supports it (parties and case number). Nothing else above it.
- SYNOPSIS (only when it has been asked for): continuous narrative prose in the register of the court, with no bullet points and no sub-headings, running from the origin of the dispute to the relief now sought and closing with the questions of law arising if the record supports them. Carry page references in brackets in the paperbook style, "(at p. 45)". Keep the length proportionate to the record.
- LIST OF DATES: an HTML table with three columns, "Date" | "Event" | "Page". One row per event. The Event column is a single crisp sentence in the past tense naming the actor and the act. No commentary, no argument and no D/P/I tags inside this table — the filed version must read as a court document.
- Then a horizontal rule and a block headed NOTES FOR COUNSEL — NOT FOR FILING (delete before filing), carrying only: (a) the coverage statement required above — what was read, what was not, and whether the List is provisional; (b) the High Court or forum convention assumed, where the record left it open; (c) the entries resting on assertion (P) or inference (I), with what would evidence each; (d) conflicting dates with both sources; (e) ambiguous, partial or ILLEGIBLE dates, the reading adopted and the source form where it differs materially from the normalised form; (f) documents referred to in the record but not on the record; (g) the limitation analysis below.

If the deliverable is "Working Chronology (internal)":
- Open with the coverage statement, then a short block headed CONFLICTS, GAPS AND LIMITATION EXPOSURE — no more than a dozen lines, each one sentence with its pin-cite, carrying only the conflicting dates, the missing links and the intervals that are close to or beyond a prescribed period. This is what counsel opens the note to find, so it goes above the table, not below it.
- Then an HTML table with five columns, "Date" | "Event" | "Source (document, page / para)" | "Basis (D/P/I)" | "Note". Include everything dated, including what looks trivial. The Note column carries the working observations counsel actually needs: a contradiction with another document, an admission, an unexplained gap in time, an act done after limitation had arguably run, a signature date that does not match the body.
- Then, under separate headings and in full: CONFLICTING DATES; GAPS IN THE RECORD (documents referred to but not produced, and periods for which there is no material at all); LIMITATION INTERVALS; OPEN QUESTIONS FOR COUNSEL.

If the deliverable is "Limitation Chronology":
- The dated events bearing on limitation only, in the same five-column table, followed by a computation table with columns "Relief / proceeding" | "Governing Article or provision (Limitation Act, 1963, or the special statute; state 'Limitation Act not applicable — writ' where it is a writ)" | "Time begins to run from (event and date)" | "Prescribed period" | "Expiry" | "Filed on (or 'not yet filed')" | "Days elapsed vs. prescribed period" | "Source". The days column carries arithmetic only — the number of days and the direction — never a conclusion that the proceeding is in time or out of time.

If something other than the above was asked for, follow the closest of these formats and adapt it.

LIMITATION, WHICHEVER FORMAT WAS CHOSEN
Compute from the dated events in the table, show the arithmetic and state the assumption behind each computation. Where a date the computation needs is not on the record, say the interval cannot be computed and name the document that would supply it; do not assume it.
Set out the intervals anyone will check first:
- cause of action to institution of the suit, naming the governing Article of the Limitation Act, 1963 where the nature of the claim identifies it;
- the impugned order to the appeal or revision;
- Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 — three intervals, which is why the drawn / presented / dishonoured / memo received rows are kept apart: presentation of the cheque within the period of its validity under proviso (a); the statutory notice given within thirty days of receipt of information from the bank of the dishonour under proviso (b); and the complaint filed within one month of the date on which the cause of action arose under clause (c) of the proviso, that is on the expiry of fifteen days from service of the notice, as required by Section 142(1)(b);
- the notice invoking arbitration under Section 21 to the application under Section 11;
- receipt of the award to a petition under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 — three months, and the further thirty days which is an outer limit and not an ordinary condonation: Section 5 of the Limitation Act, 1963 does not apply to it, so delay beyond the three months and thirty days cannot be condoned at all. Where a request under Section 33 was made to the arbitral tribunal, time runs from the date that request was disposed of and not from receipt of the award;
- appeals to the NCLAT — under Section 61(2) of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016, thirty days from the order with a further fifteen days condonable on sufficient cause, the period running from the pronouncement of the order and not from receipt of a copy; under Section 421(3) of the Companies Act, 2013, forty-five days from the date on which a copy of the order is made available to the aggrieved person, with a further forty-five days condonable. State the certified-copy position as a separate line in either case: the date the certified copy was applied for and the date it was made ready or delivered, and whether the Section 12(2) exclusion can be computed on this record at all.
WRITS. Do not name an Article of the Limitation Act, 1963 and do not state a prescribed period for a petition under Article 226 or Article 32 — the Act does not apply to such petitions. Address delay and laches instead: the date of the impugned action, the date on which the Petitioner came to know of it and the material on the record that establishes that knowledge, the explanation for the delay that appears on the record and whether it is on affidavit, any representation made and the date it was decided or rejected, and any period fixed by the special statute or the service rules under which the impugned action was taken.
EXCLUSIONS. State which the record supports and which it does not. Section 12(2), for the time taken in obtaining the certified copy, applies only where the record shows both the date of application and the date the copy was ready, so if it does not, say the exclusion cannot be computed and name the document needed; the same discipline applies to Section 14, to an acknowledgement in writing under Section 18 and to part payment under Section 19.
COVID EXCLUSION. Where a computed period touches the window from 15.03.2020 to 28.02.2022, apply the Hon'ble Supreme Court's order dated 10.01.2022 in In Re: Cognizance for Extension of Limitation in both its halves and show both computations: that period stands excluded in computing limitation; and where the balance period remaining as on 01.03.2022 is less than ninety days, the party has ninety days from 01.03.2022, that ninety-day floor being the half that usually decides whether a filing is in time.
Present the computation and what turns on it. Report the arithmetic, not the verdict: do not announce a conclusion that a claim is barred or saved — that is for counsel to settle.
"""
}

// MARK: - Caseflow

/// Caseflow — which procedural track a matter is on, and what falls due next.
///
/// Ported from the platform's `caseflow` tool definition. Its premise: Indian procedure is
/// forum-specific and a misidentified track makes every step below it wrong, so the prompt makes
/// the model classify first and show its working.
///
/// ## The one tool that needs a date
///
/// It counts days to each deadline, so it prints the date it computed against. That date is a
/// parameter rather than a call to the clock — a prompt builder that reads the wall clock cannot
/// be tested for what it says on a given day. The web computes it as the **Indian** calendar day
/// (`istDayKey()`), which is what ``ToolDate/indianFormat(_:)`` does here: an advocate's deadline
/// is an Indian date wherever the phone happens to be.
let CaseflowTool = ToolSpec(
    id: "caseflow",
    title: "Caseflow",
    short: "Stage & next steps",
    blurb: "Identify the procedural track, map it, and show where the matter stands and what "
        + "falls due next.",
    output: .document,
    inputs: [
        ToolField(
            key: "deliverable",
            label: "What do you need",
            type: .select,
            options: [
                "Full Procedural Map & Next Steps",
                "Next Steps & Deadlines Only",
                "Limitation & Condonation Position",
                "Client Status Note",
                "Counsel Briefing Note (handover)",
            ]
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "party",
            label: "Acting for",
            type: .select,
            options: [
                "Plaintiff / Petitioner / Applicant / Claimant",
                "Defendant / Respondent / Opposite Party",
                "Appellant",
                "Complainant",
                "Accused",
                "Financial or Operational Creditor",
                "Corporate Debtor / Suspended Board",
                "Not on record yet (advising)",
            ]
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "asOn",
            label: "Compute deadlines as on",
            type: .text,
            placeholder: "DD.MM.YYYY — leave blank for today"
        ),
        ToolField(
            key: "context",
            label: "Anything the record does not show",
            type: .textarea,
            placeholder: "Last order, next date, what has already been filed, the track if you "
                + "know it — or paste the order sheet here if you are not attaching files…",
            big: true
        ),
    ],
    build: buildCaseflow
)

func buildCaseflow(_ v: ToolValues, _ today: Date) -> String {
    let asOn = v.text("asOn", "")
    // A typed date wins, and is passed through EXACTLY as typed rather than parsed. The prompt
    // relies on that: where the date is ambiguous it instructs the model to say so and compute
    // nothing, which a silent reinterpretation here would defeat.
    let computeOn = asOn.isEmpty ? ToolDate.indianFormat(today) : asOn

    let contextBlock = v.has("context") ? """

WHAT THE ADVOCATE HAS ADDED (instructions, and a supplement to the record — where it conflicts with a document, the document prevails and you must flag the conflict expressly):
\(v.text("context", ""))
If a track, forum or stage has been named here, treat it as an assertion to be VERIFIED against the record, not as a given: confirm it if the record bears it out, and if the record points elsewhere say so plainly in Section 1 and name the indicator that drove your different conclusion.

""" : ""

    let asOnNote = v.has("asOn") ? """

If that date is not unambiguously DD.MM.YYYY — 08/12/2026 could be 8 December or 12 August — do not silently pick a reading. Say so at the head of Section 5, give expiry dates only, leave every "Days left" cell as "not computed — computation date ambiguous", and raise no Critical flag on that basis.
""" : ""

    return """
Work out what KIND of proceeding this matter is, which forum's procedure governs it, where it presently stands on that procedure, and what falls due next. Indian procedure is forum-specific and a misidentified track makes every step below it wrong, so classify FIRST and show your working in a form the reader can check at a glance.

Acting for: \(v.text("party", "not stated — work out from the record whom this office represents, and if the record does not show it, say so and set out both sides’ obligations separately")).

COMPUTATION DATE: \(computeOn). Treat that as today for every period, every expiry and every "days left" figure in this document, and echo it in DD.MM.YYYY at the head of Section 5. Use no other date as "today" and do not substitute a date of your own.\(asOnNote)
If for any reason you are working without a settled computation date, an expiry date standing alone is correct output: give it, write "cannot compute without a settled computation date" under Days left, and flag nothing as Critical. An invented "today" contaminates every figure below it.
\(contextBlock)
READ THE RECORD IN THIS ORDER
Find, before anything else: (a) the order sheet / daily orders / roznama; (b) the most recent dated order or listing; (c) the first page of the originating document (plaint, petition, application, complaint, memorandum of appeal) bearing the case number and the provision invoked; (d) the memo of parties; (e) proof of service. The case number and the last order settle almost everything downstream. Where one of these is not in the record, record it as a gap rather than assuming its contents.

COUNT THE PROCEEDINGS BEFORE YOU MAP THEM
Do not assume one matter equals one track. A single brief routinely carries a complaint under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881, a summary suit and an application under Section 7 of the IBC between the same parties; or a main petition with three pending interlocutory applications, a caveat and an appeal against an interim order. List every proceeding that is live on this record — main matter, connected and transferred matters, every pending IA / OA / MA, appeals and revisions, execution, and any parallel criminal or arbitral proceeding — and classify EACH to its own track with its own map, its own stage and its own clocks. Never fold an interlocutory application into the main matter's stage: the next actionable thing on a file is more often an IA than the main case, and clocks in different proceedings run in parallel.

EVIDENCE RULE — applies to every line of the output
- Every fact carries a pin-cite in brackets: (file name, p. N, para N / order dated DD.MM.YYYY). Use the real file names of the attached documents.
- Every period, deadline and consequence carries the provision that creates it (Act and section, or Order and Rule). Where you know the period but are not certain which provision creates it, state the period and mark the provision "provision to be verified" — do not manufacture a section number in order to license the period, and do not suppress a period you are sure of merely because the number escapes you. Where you are sure of neither, write "period and provision to be verified" and name where to look.
- CITE ONLY REAL, VERIFIABLE PROVISIONS AND RULES. High Court rules, Original Side rules, tribunal practice directions, circulars and prescribed form numbers are exactly what gets confabulated, as is the CrPC-to-BNSS renumbering. Where you are not certain a citation is accurate, state the requirement and mark the citation "[to be verified]" rather than producing a plausible-looking one. A correct requirement with an unverified citation is usable; an invented rule number is not.
- Anything that is your own reading rather than a fact on the record or a statutory provision must begin with "Assessment:".
- Never state a hearing date, filing date, case number or limitation trigger that is not in the record. "The record does not show X" is a correct and valuable answer; a guessed date is a liability.
- ILLEGIBLE AND POOR SCANS. Order sheets and roznamas in the district courts are scanned, often handwritten and routinely badly OCR'd. If a digit, date, figure or case number is smudged, broken or unreadable, give your best reading followed by [unclear in source] — never a clean guess — and do not run any computation off it: that row's Due by reads "cannot compute — source date unclear", and say which page needs a legible copy.
- AMBIGUOUS DATES. Render every date as DD.MM.YYYY. Where a numeric date is genuinely ambiguous between DD/MM and MM/DD (03/05/2026), resolve it only from context — the surrounding sequence, a date spelled out for the same event elsewhere, the document's own convention — and state the basis you used. If it cannot be resolved, keep the document's own form, mark it AMBIGUOUS, give the consequence of each reading, and do not compute a single deadline off it. This tool does arithmetic on these dates, so a silent reading becomes a wrong deadline.

STRUCTURE — number the sections exactly as below, as <h2> headings; render every tabulated part as a real HTML <table> with the columns named (six columns maximum, so it stays readable on the page), and the lists as <ol> / <ul>.

AT A GLANCE — above Section 1, unnumbered, a bordered two-column table (label, then value) with no heading row and five rows: Track — the proceeding and the code that governs it; Current stage; Next listed date and bench; Nearest deadline and what it is; Anything already overdue. One line each, pin-cited, and "not ascertainable from the record" where that is the honest answer. This is what a partner reads first and it must not be buried behind a twenty-row map. Where more than one proceeding is live, give one line per proceeding for Track and stage.

1. WHAT THIS MATTER IS
First, where more than one proceeding is live, a table: Proceeding | Case number and year | Forum | Track (code or statute that governs it) | Status (pending / disposed / stage unknown). Then take each live proceeding in turn below.
Table: Attribute | Finding | Source. Rows for: forum, bench and coram; case type, number and year; date of institution; parties and their array position; relief or claim pressed; the statute and provision under which the proceeding is brought; claim amount or specified value (it decides the commercial track and pecuniary jurisdiction).
Then "Why this track" — three to six sentences naming the tell-tale indicators you relied on (the case-number prefix, the provision invoked in the cause title or prayer, the statute named, the forum's own nomenclature and forms), each pin-cited.
Then "Governing procedure" — name the procedural code AND the forum's own rules that will actually be applied, marking any rule you cannot verify as above.
Then "Confidence" — High, Moderate or Cannot determine. At Moderate or below, set out the competing track(s), what distinguishes them on these facts, and the single document that would settle it. If the record does not establish the forum at all, open the section by saying so in bold and present the two most probable tracks side by side instead of picking one.
If the matter is still pre-institution (a notice issued and a reply awaited, an FIR registered with no charge-sheet, a mediation under Section 12A pending), say so — the map then begins before institution.

2. THE PROCEDURAL MAP FOR EACH TRACK
One table per live proceeding, each under an <h3> carrying that proceeding's case number, running institution to disposal and on to appeal and execution: # | Stage | Governing provision | Prescribed time | Status in this matter. Status must be exactly one of: Completed / In progress / Not yet due / Not applicable / Not ascertainable from the record. Every Completed or In progress carries its pin-cite.
The map must be the one this forum will actually apply — do not hand back a generic civil-suit map for a writ petition, an insolvency application, an arbitration, a criminal trial or a statutory appeal, and do not force a matter onto the nearest familiar track merely because it is familiar.

3. WHERE THE MATTER STANDS NOW
For each live proceeding: the current stage, taken from its table above. The most recent dated event in the record — its date, what it was, and the operative direction quoted, pin-cited. Whether that direction has been complied with: done / outstanding / not ascertainable, pin-cited. The next listed date and bench if the record shows them, and if not, say so.
Close with record staleness: state the date of the most recent document in the record and warn expressly that anything that has happened after that date is outside this analysis.

4. WHAT COMES NEXT
One table per live proceeding, under an <h3> with its case number: Step | Who must act | Provision | Due by | How computed | Consequence of default. Interlocutory applications get their own rows — a pending stay application, an Order XXXIX application, an application for condonation, an amendment application, a bail application — because they are usually the actionable next thing. Mark our side's steps "OURS". "How computed" must show the arithmetic and its starting point, e.g. "service on 04.03.2026 (Affidavit of service, p. 3) + 120 days = 02.07.2026". Where the starting date is not in the record, put "cannot compute" under Due by and name the document that would supply it.
Then, in two or three lines, which of these clocks run in parallel and which single date falls first across every proceeding, so the reader knows what to do this week without reading the tables again.
Also flag any default on the OTHER side that is apparent on the record — an unfiled written statement past its outer limit, a missing affidavit of admission and denial, an unanswered notice, non-compliance with a costs or conditional order — because that is leverage and it is actionable now.
Then a short paragraph on what to actually prepare for the immediate next step: the document, its annexures and affidavits, the court fee, and any application for condonation, leave or extension that must travel with it.

5. LIMITATION AND STATUTORY CLOCKS
Open with the computation date in DD.MM.YYYY. Then ONE table covering every proceeding, ordered by the nearest expiry first: Clock | Provision | Trigger date and source | Expires | Days left as on the computation date | Status. Name the proceeding inside the Clock cell where more than one is live ("CS(COMM) 45/2025 — written statement"). Status is exactly one of: Expired / Critical (30 days or less) / Live / Cannot compute / To be verified — the last where the period or the provision is not settled enough to state.
Cover every clock live in this matter: limitation for the claim itself, for appeal, revision or review, for execution, the periods peculiar to each track, and any timeline imposed by an order in the record.
For each, state whether the delay is condonable and under which provision — and where it is not, say so in terms. An uncondonable bar stated plainly is the single most useful line in this document.
Before writing "Expired" against any row, run the exclusions: Section 4 of the Limitation Act, 1963 where the period ended on a day the court was closed; Section 12(1) excluding the day from which the period is to be reckoned and Section 12(2) the time taken in obtaining a certified copy; Section 14 for a proceeding bona fide pursued in a wrong forum; Section 15(2) excluding a statutory notice period such as the two months under Section 80 CPC; Section 18 acknowledgment in writing and Section 19 part payment. Say which of these the record actually supports and which it does not.
Where any computed period touches the window from 15.03.2020 to 28.02.2022, apply the Hon'ble Supreme Court's suo motu exclusion of that period (In Re: Cognizance for Extension of Limitation) before stating any conclusion, noting that where the balance period remaining as on 01.03.2022 was less than 90 days, 90 days from 01.03.2022 were available. Never print "Expired" against a period touching that window without first working the exclusion and showing it — an affirmatively wrong "Expired" is worse than "cannot compute", because the file gets closed on it.
Where a trigger date is absent from the record, the row reads "cannot compute" and names the document needed. Do not estimate a limitation date.

6. EXPOSURE AND IMMEDIATE ACTIONS
A numbered list, at most seven items, most urgent first: what is already overdue or in default on the face of the record, what is at risk, and the precise step to take, each tied to a row above and to the person who must do it. Practical, this-week actions.

7. WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW
The specific documents that would complete this analysis, each with one line on which question above it would answer.

TRACK ANCHORS — a recognition checklist for reaching the right code, not a substitute for reading the record. Where the applicable version of a statute turns on the date of institution or the date of the offence, say which version you have applied and why.
- Ordinary civil suit — CPC, 1908: Order V summons, Order VIII Rule 1 written statement (30 days, extendable to 90), Order X, Order XI, Order XIV issues, Order XVIII evidence, Order XX judgment, Section 96 and Order XLI first appeal, Order XXI execution.
- Commercial suit (specified value of Rs 3 lakh or more) — Commercial Courts Act, 2015: Section 12A pre-institution mediation, mandatory unless urgent interim relief is sought; written statement within 120 days of service with forfeiture of the right thereafter (proviso to Order VIII Rule 1 as amended); Order XI disclosure on affidavit; Order XV-A case management hearing; written arguments; judgment within 90 days of conclusion of arguments; appeal to the Commercial Appellate Division within 60 days under Section 13.
- Writ — Articles 226 and 32 with the High Court's own rules: no fixed limitation but delay and laches, notice or rule, counter-affidavit and rejoinder on the Court's directions, intra-court appeal where maintainable, and an SLP under Article 136 within 90 days in a civil matter and 60 days in a criminal one (Order XXI Rule 3, Supreme Court Rules, 2013), or 60 days from the refusal of a certificate of fitness. Article 226 is invoked as often on the criminal side as the civil, so identify which before stating the SLP period.
- Insolvency before the NCLT — IBC, 2016: the default threshold of Rs 1 crore under Section 4, which decides whether an application under Section 7 or 9 is maintainable at all; Section 8 demand notice with 10 days to reply; application under Section 7 / 9 / 10; admission or rejection within 14 days under Section 7(4) / 9(5), which is directory and not mandatory (Surendra Trading), so do not report a Bench as being in default against it; moratorium under Section 14; public announcement and constitution of the CoC; CIRP of 180 days extendable by 90 under Section 12 with a 330-day outer limit; liquidation under Section 33; appeal to the NCLAT within 30 days plus 15 condonable under Section 61; appeal to the Hon'ble Supreme Court within 45 days plus 15 condonable under Section 62; NCLT Rules, 2016 throughout. Limitation on the application itself runs under Article 137 of the Limitation Act, 1963, subject to acknowledgment under Section 18.
- Company petition — Companies Act, 2013 (Sections 241-242 oppression and mismanagement, schemes, and the rest) with the NCLT Rules, 2016. Do not conflate this track with the IBC one.
- Arbitration — A&C Act, 1996: Section 21 invocation, Section 11 appointment, Section 23 statements of claim and defence, then the tribunal's own procedural orders and timetable, which govern; award within 12 months of completion of pleadings under Section 29A, extendable by 6 months by consent and thereafter only by the Court (an endeavour rather than a mandate for international commercial arbitration); correction under Section 33 within 30 days; challenge under Section 34(3) within 3 months plus 30 days condonable and not thereafter; enforcement under Section 36; appeal under Section 37.
- Criminal — WHICH CODE APPLIES IS TWO SEPARATE QUESTIONS, and mixing them is the classic error on a live file:
  (a) the OFFENCE and its punishment come from the law in force on the DATE OF THE OFFENCE — the IPC for offences before 01.07.2024, the BNS for offences on or after it, Article 20(1) of the Constitution forbidding retrospective substantive criminal law;
  (b) the PROCEDURE comes from the date the proceeding was INITIATED — Section 531(2)(a) BNSS saves only an appeal, application, trial, inquiry or investigation PENDING immediately before 01.07.2024, which continues under the CrPC; anything initiated on or after that date runs under the BNSS whenever the offence was committed. So the ordinary file — offence in March 2024, FIR registered in September 2024 — is IPC offences tried under BNSS procedure, and the charge-sheet clock, the default-bail provision and every application take their numbers from the BNSS. Evidence follows procedure, the BSA governing proceedings initiated on or after that date; where anything material turns on the changeover, flag it rather than assuming it.
  State in Section 1 which code you have applied to which question and the date that drove it. Give the BNSS provision with the CrPC equivalent in brackets; the renumbering is extensive, so where you are not certain of the corresponding number give the one you are sure of and mark the other "[to be verified]".
  Identify the KIND of criminal case before listing any stages, because the maps differ: a police case (FIR under Section 173 BNSS / 154 CrPC) runs investigation, arrest and remand, the 60 / 90-day charge-sheet clock under Section 187(3) BNSS (167(2) CrPC) with the indefeasible right to default bail on its expiry, report on completion of investigation under Section 193 BNSS (173 CrPC), cognizance, supply of documents, committal where the offence is exclusively triable by the Court of Session, discharge or framing of charge, prosecution evidence, examination of the accused, defence evidence, arguments, judgment. A private complaint runs differently — examination of the complainant on oath under Section 223 BNSS (200 CrPC), with the BNSS proviso that no cognizance may be taken without giving the proposed accused an opportunity of being heard, a step with no CrPC analogue and one regularly missed; inquiry under Section 225 BNSS (202 CrPC) where the accused resides outside jurisdiction; then issue of process or dismissal. Summons case, warrant case, sessions trial and summary trial each carry their own sequence and their own discharge provision (including the BNSS windows of 60 days from commitment, and 60 days from the supply of documents in a warrant case on a police report) — name which applies and why.
  Where the party is an accused, bail is normally the most time-critical item and must appear in Sections 4 and 5: anticipatory bail under Section 482 BNSS (438 CrPC), regular bail under Sections 480 and 483 BNSS (437 and 439 CrPC), default bail on the expiry of the charge-sheet period, release of an undertrial under Section 479 BNSS (436A CrPC), the special bail bars of any special Act in play (Section 37 NDPS, Section 45 PMLA, Section 43D(5) UAPA), cancellation, and compliance with every condition already imposed.
  Limitation for taking cognizance: Chapter XXXVIII BNSS (Sections 467-473 CrPC) — six months, one year or three years by reference to the punishment, with no bar for the graver offences. Post-trial: appeal against conviction within 30 days to the Court of Session and 60 days to the High Court, and against acquittal within 90 days (Articles 115 and 114, Limitation Act, 1963), revision, quashing under Section 528 BNSS (482 CrPC), and a criminal SLP within 60 days.
- Cheque dishonour — Section 138 NI Act: notice within 30 days of the return memo, 15 days to pay, complaint within one month of the expiry of that period under Section 142, interim compensation under Sections 143A and 148.
- Consumer — Consumer Protection Act, 2019. Fix the forum first, because that is the determination this track exists to make: pecuniary jurisdiction goes by the value of the goods or services PAID as consideration and not by the compensation claimed (Section 34(1)) — District Commission up to Rs 50 lakh, State Commission above Rs 50 lakh and up to Rs 2 crore, National Commission above Rs 2 crore; territorial jurisdiction under Section 34(2), including the complainant's own residence or place of work. Complaint within 2 years of the cause of action under Section 69, condonable for sufficient cause; reply within 30 days extendable by 15 and no further; appeal to the State Commission within 45 days under Section 41 and to the National Commission within 30 days under Section 51, each with the 50 per cent pre-deposit of the amount ordered; appeal to the Hon'ble Supreme Court within 30 days under Section 67.
- Secured-debt enforcement — SARFAESI, 2002: Section 13(2) notice with 60 days, reply under Section 13(3A), measures under Section 13(4), application to the DRT within 45 days under Section 17, appeal to the DRAT within 30 days under Section 18 with the pre-deposit; original applications under the RDB Act before the DRT.
- Motor accident claims — Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 before the Claims Tribunal: application under Section 166, and the six-month bar in Section 166(3) reintroduced by the 2019 amendment and in force from 01.04.2022, which is a genuine trap because such claims previously had no limitation — check the date of the accident against that commencement before saying anything about limitation; the detailed accident report and the insurer's offer under Sections 159 and 149; no-fault compensation under Section 164; appeal to the High Court within 90 days under Section 173, with the deposit condition in its proviso.
- Real estate — RERA, 2016: complaint to the Authority under Section 31, adjudicating officer for compensation under Section 71, relief under Section 18, appeal to the Appellate Tribunal within 60 days under Section 44 with the promoter's pre-deposit under the proviso to Section 43(5), and appeal to the High Court within 60 days under Section 58.
- Rent and eviction — the State Rent Act before the Rent Controller or Small Causes Court, with its own leave-to-defend window in a summary eviction petition; where the premises fall outside the Rent Act, a civil suit preceded by a notice to quit under Section 106 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882. Name the State enactment; do not apply another State's.
- Industrial and labour — Industrial Disputes Act, 1947: conciliation, reference or direct approach to the Labour Court, the three-year limitation for an application under Section 2A(3), recovery under Section 33C(2), an award becoming enforceable on the expiry of 30 days from publication under Section 17A, and the bar in Section 33 during pendency. Check whether the labour codes have been brought into force for the provision in question before applying them, and say which you have applied.
- Land acquisition — RFCTLARR Act, 2013: notification, declaration and award with their statutory outer limits and the consequence of lapse, reference to the Authority under Section 64, and appeal to the High Court within 60 days under Section 74; for acquisitions under the Land Acquisition Act, 1894, the reference under its Section 18.
- Probate, succession and testamentary — Indian Succession Act, 1925: petition under Section 276, citations, caveat and the conversion into a testamentary suit, succession certificate under Section 372, and the correct testamentary forum. The right to apply for probate accrues when it becomes necessary to apply, so treat delay as something to be explained rather than counted mechanically.
- Execution — a proceeding in its own right and not the tail of the suit, with its own map, its own limitation and its own next date: Order XXI CPC, application under Rule 11, notice under Rule 22, attachment and sale, exemptions under Section 60, transfer of the decree under Section 39, and enforcement of an arbitral award as a decree under Section 36 of the A&C Act, 1996. Limitation is 12 years from when the decree became enforceable (Article 136), and 3 years for a decree granting a mandatory injunction (Article 135).
- Service, tax, environmental and other tribunals — the constituting statute, the appeal period it prescribes, and that tribunal's own rules and prescribed forms.
- ANY TRACK NOT LISTED ABOVE. This list is a starting point and not a universe. Where the matter does not fall within it, say so in terms and build the map from the statute that constitutes the proceeding and from that forum's own rules, marking anything you cannot verify. Do not force the matter onto the nearest listed track: a confidently wrong map is the failure this whole document exists to prevent.
- Limitation across every track — Limitation Act, 1963: Section 5 condonation (not available for a suit), Section 4 where the period expires on a day the court is closed, Section 12(1) and 12(2) exclusions, Section 14 for proceedings bona fide pursued in a wrong forum, Section 15(2) for a statutory notice period, Sections 18 and 19 acknowledgment and part payment, Articles 116 and 117 for appeals, Article 137 residuary three years, Articles 135 and 136 for execution.

TAILOR THE OUTPUT TO THE DELIVERABLE — produce: \(v.text("deliverable", "Full Procedural Map & Next Steps")). AT A GLANCE appears in every variant.
- "Full Procedural Map & Next Steps": all seven sections in full.
- "Next Steps & Deadlines Only": Section 1 compressed to the classification table and a two-line "why this track"; omit Section 2; then Sections 3 to 7 in full.
- "Limitation & Condonation Position": this is a forward-looking clock list and not a chronology — do not produce a dated narrative of what has happened. Section 1 compressed, Section 3 cut to the current stage and the last order, then Section 5 at full depth: every clock still running or already run out, computed, pin-cited, with the condonation position, the applicable exclusions and the consequence of missing it — then Sections 6 and 7. Say for each expired clock whether it can still be revived and on what application, because that is the question being asked.
- "Client Status Note": the same substance addressed to a lay client. Plain English, short sentences, no Latin; name the governing Act once and describe each step thereafter in ordinary words. What has happened, where the matter stands, what happens next and roughly when, what the client must supply and by when, and what is at risk. Keep the pin-cites in brackets so the advocate can check the note before it goes out. No opinion on merits and no prediction of outcome.
- "Counsel Briefing Note (handover)": written for an advocate picking this brief up cold. Sections 1, 2 and 3 tight, then 4, 5 and 6, and Section 7 recast as "the file is missing the following". Close with a short "watch-outs" paragraph on the traps peculiar to this matter — a forfeited right, an unserved party, a conditional order, an undertaking given to the Hon'ble Court, an interim order about to lapse.

IF NO RECORD IS SUPPLIED
If nothing is attached and nothing has been pasted, do not invent a case, parties, case number or dates. Say in one line that no record was supplied. Then, if the track is identifiable from what the advocate has typed, give Sections 2 and 5 as a GENERIC map and clock-list for that track, clearly labelled "General map — not matter-specific", and replace AT A GLANCE and Sections 1, 3, 4 and 6 with one line each explaining why they cannot be answered. Close with Section 7: what to attach, beginning with the order sheet and the latest order. If text has been pasted instead of files, treat that text as the record and cite it as "(pasted text)" — never under a document name you have invented.

READ THIS LAST — IT OVERRIDES ANY GENERAL FORMATTING DIRECTION THAT FOLLOWS
A direction to make reasonable professional assumptions and to mark missing information with [bracketed placeholders] is a convention for documents being composed. This document is not composed, it is reported from a record. That direction therefore does not apply to any date, deadline, case number, party name, provision, forum, stage or limitation trigger here. For those there is no assumption, no [placeholder] and no plausible substitute: write that the record does not show it and name the document that would. This override governs the whole of this output.
"""
}
